var MANIFEST = [{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","page":"index.html","group":"Start","text":" \n   write games as components. \n   tse is a native-first 2d engine that you write in react-like tsx. there is no react and no virtual dom: a compiler turns each component into a typescript class whose children are persistent instances, updated in place when state changes. \n\n   \n      component.tsx your tsx: state, hooks, elements.  \u2192 \n      compiler checks the rules, lowers to an ir, emits a class.  \u2192 \n      generated .ts one class per component, committed next to an  .ir.json .  \u2192 \n      player runs the frame loop, draws via raylib, reads input. \n   \n\n   the mental model in five lines \n   \n     a component is a function: hooks, then exactly one  return  of jsx. it is compiled, never run as written. \n     its root is a built-in ( entity ,  sprite ,  game \u2026), which the generated class  extends . \n     state is written directly ( s.hp -= 1 ), and a handler reads its own writes; render sees what the frame committed. all of a frame's writes cause one render. \n     position is state:  x={s.x} y={s.y}  on any built-in (wrap a component in  <entity x y>  to place it), usually driven by  usemutable . rows and columns are a  <flex> , anywhere. see  placing things . \n     each frame runs  useinput , then  useupdate , then renders dirty components, commits structure and draws. \n   \n\n    game code goes in components, as in react. an enemy keeps its own  hp  in  usemutable , walks in its  useupdate , takes hits in its collider's  oncollisionenter  and tells its parent it died through a callback prop. a plain function in a  .ts  file is for what you would extract in react too: a pure calculation, a table, or something a search runs thousands of times.  \n\n    anything outside the supported subset is a compiler error ( tse#### ), never silently ignored. see  error codes .  \n\n   a whole component \n  \nimport { usemutable, useinput, useupdate, entity, sprite, collider } from \"@tsengine/runtime/jsx\";\nimport { warningicon } from \"./warningicon.tsx\";\n\nexport function player() {\n  const s = usemutable({ x: 0, health: 100 });\n\n  useinput((input) => {\n    if (input.pressed(\"space\")) s.health -= 10;\n  });\n  useupdate((dt) => {\n    s.x += 60 * dt;                         // 60 px per second\n  });\n\n  return (\n     \n        50 ? \"normal.png\" : \"hurt.png\"} />\n       \n      {s.health  }\n     \n  );\n}\n  \n "},{"id":"react","title":"Coming from React","page":"index.html","group":"Start","text":" \n   coming from react \n   a component reads like react, and most of what you know carries over. what differs is that it is compiled, never run as written: the compiler turns it into a class whose children live as long as it does and are updated in place. the function body is a description for the compiler, which understands only the part of typescript it can turn into that class. anything else is an error ( tse#### ) when you build. \n\n   what carries over \n    \n      react  here  \n      function components, props, a props type  the same. the props type is written in the parameter ( ({ hp }: { hp: number }) ) or declared in the same file.  \n      an early  if (!hero) return null;   the same, at the top of the body: each  if (\u2026) return \u2026;  becomes a condition of the last  return , and what follows it is worked out only when it did not return.  \n       usestate , updaters, batching   usemutable : state you write directly ( s.n += 1 ), with no setters or updaters. a handler reads its own writes at once, so  s.n += 1  twice adds 2; render sees what the frame committed, and any number of writes cause one render per frame. a  usestate  is an error whose message writes out the conversion in your names. why:  design.md, \"state\" .  \n       usememo ,  usecallback ,  react.memo   not needed. the compiler knows what every value reads and does their job itself. a derived  const  that calls a function is worked out again only when what it reads changes. a render writes an element's values, or sends a child its props, only when a state or prop they read has a different value than at the last render. the component's functions are made once, so passing one down never re-renders anyone. see  what a render does .  \n       {cond && <x />} ,  {a ? <x /> : <y />} , fragments  the same. a condition turning off unmounts.  \n       {items.map((i) => <x key={i.id} />)}   the same, with one inline arrow. instances follow keys.  key  on a single element starts it over when it changes.  \n       children    children: child  in the props type, placed once as  {children} .  \n      custom hooks  the same, written as  use\u2026  functions; each call is expanded in place, so it gets its own state.  \n      a zustand store   creategamestate , read with  usegame((s) => \u2026)  selectors and changed by actions.  \n    \n\n   what is different, and what to write instead \n    \n      you would reach for  write  \n       useeffect   there is none, on purpose. do the work where the change happens (an action, a handler), derive values, and use  usemount , which may return a cleanup. see  no useeffect .  \n       onkeydown ,  onclick   keys are polled:  useinput((input) => input.pressed(\"jump\"))  every frame, and  useupdate((dt) => \u2026)  for the step. clicks are  onpress  on a  <pointer>  or  <flex> .  inert  and  modal  decide which subtrees hear the keys. see  who hears the keys .  \n       style , css positioning   x ,  y ,  rotation ,  z  and  opacity  props, relative to the parent;  <flex>  lays out ui.  \n       usecontext   the same:  createcontext  in a  .ts  file,  <theme.provider value> ,  usecontext(theme) . what the whole game shares goes in  usegame .  \n       ref={\u2026}  on an element  none: what is on screen follows state and props. a value to keep is a  usemutable  field.  \n      text in jsx:  <p>score {n}</p>   inside  <text> , as its children:  <text>score {n}</text> , or a component of your own that takes text ( <label>score {n}</label> , see  text wrappers ). there is no  text  attribute.  \n      a loop or  push  in the body to build a list  the same: statements in the body (loops,  let , a local array) run again when what they read changes.  \n    \n\n   three places code can go \n   each place is checked by its own rules, and most \"why is this an error\" questions come from code written in the wrong one. \n    \n      where  may hold  for  \n      the component body  hooks, then statements that work out what to draw (derived  const s, loops,  let ), and one  return  of jsx. it writes no state: the body describes the screen and changes nothing.  describing what is on screen, from the component's state and props.  \n      handlers:  useinput ,  useupdate ,  usemount , collisions, callback props, the component's own functions  statements that write state, call actions and play sounds (see  what handler code may contain ).  the game: what happens on a key, a step, a contact or a press.  \n      plain  .ts  files  any typescript.  what you would extract in react too: tables and numbers, a pure calculation (a path, a target), the game state, and code a search runs many times (kite's solver).  \n    \n    hearth keeps its battle in components:  play  keeps the gold, the lives and the lists; each  enemy ,  tower  and  shot  keeps its own state and steps itself; they report up with callbacks and reach each other through collisions that carry data. each is tested alone with   @tsengine/testing  . kite keeps its movement in plain functions because a solver steps it thousands of times. see  plain functions .  \n\n   when something goes wrong \n   each component is written out as a class in  generated/  (by  tse dev ,  build  and  check ; never edit it). you should not have to read it, because an error names your  .tsx . \n    \n      what  where it points  \n      a rule of the language ( tse1xxx )  the line in  src/ , as you wrote it (custom hooks included).  \n      a type error ( tse9001 ,  tse9002 )  the line in  src/  of the element or hook; for one found in the generated code, where it was found is added in brackets.  \n      an error thrown while the game runs (in the window, in a test)   at main.oninput (src/main.tsx:14:3) : the component's class, the method its hook became ( oninput ,  onupdate ,  onmount ,  render ) and the line of that hook or element. plain functions in  .ts  files show their own line.  \n    \n   when the game does something you did not expect \n    \n      what you see  why, and what to write  \n      my enemy does not see the player  two components share a value through their common parent: the player reports where it is with a callback prop, the parent keeps it in  usemutable  and hands it down (a prop, or context). it arrives one step later, every time. see  sharing state between components .  \n      my collision is a step late (the hero is inside the wall before it stops)   oncollisionenter  runs after the step's updates. move with   usebody  :  move  stops at walls, and  touching  and  overlapping  answer in the same step.  \n      the game is slow, or a frame takes too long  under  tse dev , open the inspector and press  profile . it shows milliseconds per frame for each component and each phase of the frame, slowest first ( the inspector ).  \n      a box is not where i expect, or i cannot see what a component holds  under  tse dev , press  f1  in the window to outline every box, its padding, every collider and pointer, and to name the box under the mouse. to watch the tree, a component's state and the game state as they change, open the inspector at the address  tse dev  prints. see  seeing inside a running game .  \n      something went wrong a few seconds ago, and is hard to make happen again  under  tse dev , press  f2  and step back to it with the arrow keys. fix the code and save, and the same moment plays again with the fix.  f4  saves it for a test. see  rewind .  \n      i am building a panel or a dialogue box, and must play to where it shows each time  give it a stories file (its props, and game state for what it reads), and  tse preview  shows it on its own with hot reload;  tse preview --screenshot  stores pictures that  tse check  compares. see  previews .  \n    \n    tse  turns this on for you (source maps next to each generated file, and node's  --enable-source-maps  for the window and for  tse check 's tests). a packaged game, and the rust host ( --engine quickjs ), do not map yet: their errors name the bundled  game.js . \n "},{"id":"quickstart","title":"Quick start","page":"index.html","group":"Start","text":" \n   quick start \n   requires node.js \u2265 22.18. \n  \nnpm install\nnpm test               # full pipeline tests\nnpm run typecheck      # tsc over compiler, runtime, tests and generated output\n\n# compile components:    \nnode packages/compiler/src/cli.ts games/demo/src games/demo/generated\nnpm run demo           # same, for the demo\nnpm run example        # recompile the example and check its scenes\n\n# the native player (needs cmake + a c compiler; x11/gl dev packages on linux)\nnpm run native:build   # libtse with a real window\nnpm run play:demo      # play the demo under node\nnpm run check:    # headless: compile, type-check, test and replay a game package\nnpm run screenshot     # render chosen steps of a scripted run to png\n\n# ship it\nnpm run package                          # node single-executable, ~125 mb, fast script\nnpm run package -- --engine quickjs      # rust + quickjs host, a few mb, no jit\nnpm run package -- --engine web          # a page for a browser or a phone, webgl2\n  \n   project setup \n   put components in  .tsx  files in one folder, with this  tsconfig.json : \n  \n{\n  \"compileroptions\": {\n    \"jsx\": \"react-jsx\",\n    \"jsximportsource\": \"@tsengine/runtime\"\n  }\n}\n  \n   the compiler cli \n  \ntse-compile     [--runtime  ] [--no-check] [--scenes  ] [--assets  ]\n  \n    \n      flag  effect  \n       --no-check   skip both type-checks (source  tse9002 , generated  tse9001 ).  \n       --scenes <dir>   validate every  .json  scene against the compiled prefabs, and import every tiled  .tmj  map to  <outdir>/scenes/<name>.scene.json .  \n       --assets <dir>   check that every literal image, font, music and  usesound  path is a file in the folder ( tse1211 ).  \n       --runtime <module>   where generated code imports the runtime from.  \n    \n   output: a  .ts  and  .ir.json  per component, plus  prefabs.ts  and  prefabs.json  (only when every file compiled). ci fails if committed generated output is stale, so  commit regenerated output . \n "},{"id":"layout","title":"Repository layout","page":"index.html","group":"Start","text":" \n   repository layout \n    \n      package  what it is  \n       packages/compiler   tsx \u2192 ir \u2192 typescript. parser, analyzer, transform, generator, type-check, diagnostics.  \n       packages/runtime   the authoring entry point ( @tsengine/runtime/jsx ), the built-in elements,  world ,  gameobject ,  creategamestate ,  loadscene ,  sound .  \n       packages/player   pure-typescript half of the player: object tree \u2192 draw records, collisions, layout, the frame loop.  \n       packages/player-node   node host: koffi binding to  libtse , and  play.ts .  \n       host/ ,  native/   the rust + quickjs-ng host and the c library  libtse  (raylib window, audio, input).  \n       games/kite   a precision platformer: a title screen, eight tiled rooms and an end screen, with music, effects and gamepad. it is the best reference for a real game; read  src/main.tsx  first.  \n       games/demo   a small playable game, the simplest end-to-end example.  \n       packages/example   example components that exercise every feature, with committed generated output and scenes.  \n    \n   design notes:  docs/design.md  (architecture, ir, decisions),  docs/layout.md  (flex, hud, screen),  docs/player.md  (player and abi),  docs/next_priorities.md  (roadmap). \n "},{"id":"decide","title":"What do I use for\u2026?","page":"index.html","group":"Start","text":" \n   what do i use for\u2026? \n  \n    \n      i want to\u2026  use  \n      keep a value that changes and shows on screen    usemutable   (per component) or   usegame   (shared by the game)  \n      keep a value that must  not  re-render (timers, held direction)  a   usemutable  field  the jsx does not read ( s.timer -= dt )  \n      move something   x={s.x} y={s.y}  driven by state, changed in   useupdate    \n      react to keys or the mouse position    useinput   with  input.down / pressed / released   \n      do something every frame    useupdate((dt) => \u2026)    \n      do something once when it appears or disappears    usemount   /  useunmount   \n      play a sound    usesound(\"hit.wav\")  , called in a handler  \n      draw an image / animation / text / debug box   sprite  /  animatedsprite  /  text  /  shape   \n      detect that two things touched    collider   with  oncollisionenter  /  oncollisionexit , plus  layer  and  hits   \n      a clickable thing in the world    pointer   +  onpress   \n      a button or panel on screen (score, health, menus)    hud  +  flex  , with  flex onpress  for buttons  \n      follow the player   <camera><player /></camera>   \n      a fixed pixel-art resolution   <screen width height>  inside  <game>   \n      window title, size, background, assets folder  props of   <game>    \n      spawn and remove many of the same thing  a  keyed list  over array state  \n      show or hide something   {cond && <x />}  or  {cond ? <a /> : <b />}   \n      let a parent tell a child something happened  a callback prop ( ondefeated ); see  props   \n      wrap whatever the parent passes (a frame, a panel)   children: child  and  {children}   \n      switch between screens (title, level, game over)    router  +  route    \n      draw a tile level, and ask what is solid    tilemap  and  gettilemap    \n      dust, trails, snow    particles   (continuous) or  createparticles  +  particlelayer  (a burst at a moment)  \n      fade the screen, play music    fade  in a  hud ,  music  in the root   \n      camera shake, squash and stretch    useshake ,  usesquash    \n      freeze frames on a hit   freeze(steps)  in a handler  \n      many things that each act on their own (enemies, towers, shots)  components that keep their own state in a keyed list, reporting up by callbacks: see hearth ( games/hearth )  \n      logic a search runs thousands of times (a solver)  plain functions in a  .ts  file, called from a component: see  kite   \n      name actions ( \"jump\" ) instead of keys    createcontrols  +  <game controls>    \n      reuse logic, or call real typescript (loops, tables)   custom hooks and plain functions   \n      animate from a sprite sheet   <animatedsprite sheet animations animation>   \n      place levels from data, or import from tiled   scenes and prefabs   \n    \n "},{"id":"components","title":"Components","page":"language.html","group":"Language","text":" \n   components \n   a component is a top-level  function  or  const  arrow with a capitalised name, optionally exported (one per module may be the default, and it needs a name). its body, in order: \n   \n     hooks:  usemutable ,  usesound ,  usegame(...) , and the handler hooks. \n     derived values:  const hurt = health < 25; , and destructured ones,  const { x, y } = heroof(run);  or  const [a, , c] = trio;  (plain names: no defaults, rests or nesting). one that calls a function or a method is worked out once and again only when the state or props it reads change, as with  usememo ; one that only combines values is written where it is used. \n     what a render does: it compares each state field, prop and game-state value the component's elements read with what it was at the last render ( === ), and works out and writes only the values of the elements that read one that changed. an enemy that moves every step rewrites its position, not its sprite's tint or its health bar's props. so there is no  usememo ,  usecallback  or  react.memo  to write: the compiler sees what each value reads. a value changes when it is a different value. a  usemutable  field changed in place ( s.items.push(item) ) is written for you as a new value (see   usemutable  ); a prop's object or the game state is changed by making a new one, never in place, as in react. what is under a condition or in a list is worked out on every render, and so is a value that calls one of the component's own functions or a callback prop, whose reads the compiler does not follow. \n     one  return  of jsx, a condition of it ( on ? <spark /> : null ,  open && <menu /> ) or  null . \n   \n   an arrow with an expression body returns that expression:  const dot = () => <sprite image=\"dot.png\" /> . \n   beside the components, a file may hold constants and helper functions, as a react file does:  const row = { bolt: 0, frost: 2 } as const; ,  function labelof(kind: kind) { \u2026 } . render expressions may index them ( row[kind] * 3 ) and use  math.pi . a top-level  let , or a constant holding jsx or reading the game state, is  tse1001 . \n\n   the root \n   the root (or a fragment  <>\u2026</> , an implicit  entity ) is usually a built-in. the class extends it, so the root's props apply to the component itself. the root may also be another component ( return <panel title=\"build\">\u2026</panel> ), a condition or  null : the component is then a bare container that  <flex>  layout sees through, so a panel it returns still grows to the column it sits in. \n   a root  <entity> ,  <game>  or fragment with no  name  is named after the component:  function player()  returning  <entity>  is  <entity name=\"player\"> , which is what a collision's  other.owner  reads. write  name  only to say something else (an id, in a list). nested entities are not named for you. \n  \n// this class extends sprite: the component *is* a sprite.\nconst coin = ({ value }: coinprops) =>  = 5 ? \"gold.png\" : \"coin.png\"} />;\n  \n   state and props may use any name, including one the root built-in has: a  usemutable  called  name  on an  entity  root, a  width  on a  collider  root. the compiler keeps such a state in a field of its own ( $name ), so the entity's own  name  is untouched. \n\n   imports \n   \n     hooks, elements,  clamp  and the  child  type: from  \"@tsengine/runtime/jsx\"  ( import { type child } ). \n     components: from  \"./name.tsx\" , named or default. no namespace imports or  as . \n     game state:  usegame  from  \"./gamestate.ts\" . other relative  .ts  imports are plain functions and constants (see  below ), by name or whole ( import * as k from \"./constants.ts\" , read as  k.speed  and called as  k.statsof(\u2026) ); relative  .tsx  imports are components or custom hooks. \n     imports are mandatory: an unimported hook or element is  tse1005 . \n      math  needs no import. \n   \n\n   allowed in a component body \n   \n      allowed  \n       number, string, boolean,  null  and  undefined  ( gate !== undefined ) \n       state and prop reads \n       unary  ! - + , binary arithmetic, comparison, logical \n        a ? b : c , parentheses, template literals \n       array and object literals, with spreads \n       member reads ( e.id ,  items.length ,  found?.hp ) \n       non-mutating array methods ( map ,  filter ,  some ,  flatmap ,  join ,  at \u2026), and  reduce , and the copying forms  tosorted ,  toreversed ,  tospliced ,  with ,  findlast .  sort  and  reverse  on an array made right there ( [...list].sort(byx) ,  list.filter(f).reverse() ) sort a copy, so they are written as  tosorted  and  toreversed  \n       methods of strings and numbers that change nothing ( padstart ,  startswith ,  slice ,  touppercase ,  split ,  replace ,  tofixed \u2026), and  string(n) ,  number(s) ,  boolean(x)  \n       calling a function from a relative  .ts  file or the same file ( spotsof(run) ): assumed pure \n       statements that compute:  let , loops,  if , a list built up with  push ,  new map() , as a handler may. a run of them is worked out together, and again only when what it reads changes; the names it declares ( const  or  let , one name each) are values after it:\n  \nconst rows: row[] = [];\nlet total = 0;\nfor (const item of items) {\n  if (item.count === 0) continue;\n  rows.push({ id: item.id, label: `${item.name} x${item.count}` });\n  total += item.count;\n}\nreturn  {rows.map((row) =>  {row.label} )} ;\n  \n      they run during render, so they cannot write state, play a sound, call an action or  return  early. \n       functions, declared as react code declares its handlers:  const add = (n: number) => { s.points += n; }; . such a function is one of the component's handlers: call it in a handler or a callback ( useupdate(() => add(1)) ), pass it to an element ( onpress={press} ), to a child or to a hook. it is not called while rendering, since it may write state. \n      \n      not allowed  \n       anywhere:  var ,  await ,  this  \n       methods that change a prop in place, or a state value outside a  usemutable  field ( push ,  sort \u2026 on them): build a new value; on a list a block or handler made itself, and on a field of a  usemutable , they are fine \n       text children and spread attributes in jsx \n        useeffect ,  ref={\u2026}  \n       hooks inside conditions, loops or handlers \n       passing the bare  props  object on or spreading it \n      \n   \n "},{"id":"hooks","title":"Hooks","page":"language.html","group":"Language","text":" \n   hooks \n   hooks are top-level statements only ( tse1101 ). any other  use*  call is  tse1102 . all come from  @tsengine/runtime/jsx  except  usegame . \n\n    \n      hook  signature  when it runs  \n       usemutable   const s = usemutable({ vx: 0 })  state, written directly ( s.vx += 1 ): a handler reads its own writes; the frame's writes render once  \n       useinput   useinput((input) => \u2026)  every frame, first  \n       useupdate   useupdate((dt) => \u2026)  every frame, after  useinput   \n       usemount   usemount(() => \u2026)  once, when joining the world (children first); may return a cleanup  \n       useunmount   useunmount(() => \u2026)  once, when leaving the world (parent first)  \n       usesound   const play = usesound(\"hit.wav\")  you call  play()  in a handler  \n       usesquash   const squash = usesquash(1.75)  shipped hook: a scale ( squash.x ,  squash.y ) that springs back to 1  \n       useshake   const shake = useshake()  shipped hook: a camera shake that runs down  \n       usetime   const time = usetime()  shipped hook: seconds since mount, for blinking and bobbing  \n       useinputdevice   const device = useinputdevice()  shipped hook:  \"keyboard\" ,  \"pointer\"  or  \"gamepad\" , by the last press  \n       usewindowsize   const window = usewindowsize()  shipped hook: the window's  width  and  height , in logical pixels  \n       usegame   usegame((s) => s.player.hp)  re-renders when that leaf changes  \n    \n\n    usemutable    \n   a component's state: an object of fields you write directly. each field renders when it changes, is saved, and is kept by hot reload and rewind. a handler reads its own writes at once, so a step needs no local copies and no setters; render sees what the frame committed. it replaces react's  usestate : a  usestate  is  tse1102 , whose message writes out the conversion in your names. \n  \nconst s = usemutable({\n  health: 100,                 // number (a literal widens to number)\n  name: \"ada\",                 // string\n  at: { x: 0, y: 0 },          // { x: number; y: number }, as typescript infers it\n  spawns: [] as spawn[],       // an empty array needs its type\n});\n\nuseupdate((dt) => {\n  s.health -= 10 * dt;\n  if (s.health  {s.name}: {s.health} ;    // render reads the committed value\n  \n   \n     form:  const s = usemutable({ a: 0, b: false }) , one object literal of plain  name: value  fields ( tse1103 ). a literal of numbers, strings and booleans (and objects and non-empty arrays of them) gets the type typescript would infer; an empty array, or a value whose type cannot be read from it, takes its type with  as  ( spawns: [] as spawn[] , the type declared in the same file), else  tse1104 . \n     the initial values are taken when the component is made: a constant, a prop, a value of the game state, or a field of a  usemutable  declared above it, as in react's first render. a per-instance object with methods may be a field ( body: createbody(options) ), its methods called through it ( s.body.move(1, 0) ). \n     a handler and render see a field at different moments. a handler ( useupdate ,  useinput , a callback prop, a function in the body) sees a field as it was last written, this step included, and so do later handlers in the same step. render, and the elements and props it makes, see what the step committed. a derived  const  that reads a field follows the same rule: in a handler it is worked out from the fields as they are now. so does a prop: a handler reads  health  as the parent has it now, which includes what a component before it in the tree wrote this step (parents first, then siblings in order). children made in a list (found again by key), the components below a list item, an item that switches between two elements, and one made with a  key  read their props live the same way, and so does a value passed by context. only a callback prop is not live: it is a function, made once. \n     fields are written in handlers only:  = ,  += ,  -= ,  *= ,  /= ,  %= ,  ||= ,  &&= ,  ??= ,  ++ ,  -- . not in the component body ( tse1402 ) or in  useunmount . a write of the same value ( === ) renders nothing. \n     a list or object field can be changed in place.  s.list.push(x) ,  pop ,  shift ,  unshift ,  splice ,  sort ,  reverse  (each a statement of its own),  s.list[i].hp -= 1 ,  s.pos.x = 3  and  s.list[i].tags.push(t)  are written for you as the new value the field would be given ( s.list = [...s.list, x] ,  s.list.with(i, { ...s.list[i], hp: \u2026 }) ), so only what the path passes through is copied, and render and other components compare values as before. a handler reads the change back at once. not supported, with a message that says what to write: a result used as a value ( const n = s.list.push(x) ), an index that is not a plain value ( s.list[next()] ),  ?. , and a change through another name ( const e = s.list[0]; e.hp = 0 , or a loop over a copy,  for (const e of s.list.filter(\u2026)) e.hp = 0 ): write  s.list = s.list.map(\u2026)  there. a loop over the field itself may write through its item:  for (const e of s.list) { e.hp -= 1; }  is written as a loop over the index, each write  s.list[i].hp -= 1 , and a read of  e  after it sees the change. such a loop cannot also change the list itself ( push ,  splice , a new  s.list ): change the items in the loop and the list after it. each write copies the list, so for hundreds of items, build the new list with  map  and assign it once. an index write needs an array; a record is replaced with a spread. \n     a list field is a  mutablelist<t> : the forms above type-check, but it is not assignable to  t[] , so a helper that takes  foe[]  and changes it in place is an error. take  readonly foe[]  to read a list, or return the new one. \n     in a handler,  s  passed whole is the component's own object for that  usemutable , made the first time a handler passes it and kept for the component's life: a helper that writes it changes the component's fields, and the handler reads them back at once, as if it had written  s.hp -= 3  itself.\n  function takehit(target: { hp: number; hits: number }, damage: number) {\n  target.hp -= damage;   // writes the enemy's field: it renders\n  target.hits++;\n}\n\nuseupdate(() => {\n  if (s.burning) takehit(s, 1);\n  if (s.hp <= 0) ondefeated?.();   // reads the hit at once\n});  \n    in render (an element's props,  <camera shake={shake}> ,  {label(s)} ) it is a copy of the fields as the frame committed them, typed  readonly : render writes nothing. a list field of the object is still a  mutablelist , so a helper that changes it in place is an error (as above); a helper gives the field a new list ( target.foes = [...target.foes, foe] ). a write through the object after the component is unmounted (a helper that kept it) does nothing and warns once in development.  const t = s  is another name for the same fields, and a custom hook hands its state back with  return s : the caller writes  counter.n += 1 . \n     a callback under a condition on a field ( {s.waiting !== null && <prompt onkey={\u2026} />} ) reads a  const waiting = s.waiting  taken above, as typescript keeps a  const 's narrowing inside a closure and not a field's. \n     in a test,  t.state().s.vx  reads a field; on a compiled component,  component.s.vx  reads it and  component.s.vx = 1  writes it. \n   \n\n   a value that never renders \n   there is no  useref : like  usestate , it is  tse1102 , and its message writes out the conversion. a timer, an accumulator or a previous value is a  usemutable  field that the jsx does not read. a write marks the component, but a render only redoes the parts that read a changed field, so a field nothing reads changes nothing that is drawn. \n  \nconst s = usemutable({ seconds: 0, timer: 0 });   // timer changes every frame; nothing reads it\n\nuseupdate((dt) => {\n  s.timer += dt;\n  if (s.timer >= 1) {\n    s.timer = 0;\n    s.seconds += 1;               // the text that shows seconds changes once a second\n  }\n});\n  \n   a field cannot be written in  useunmount : the component is going away. tell the parent with a callback prop. \n\n    useinput  and  inputstate  \n   input is polled each frame. there are no key-event props. \n    \n      member  type  meaning  \n       input.pressed(key)   boolean  true only on the frame the key went down  \n       input.down(key)   boolean  true while the key is held  \n       input.released(key)   boolean  true only on the frame the key went up  \n       input.pointerx ,  input.pointery   number  mouse position  \n    \n   key names (an unknown name warns once and never fires): \n    \n\n    useupdate  \n    dt  is the time in  seconds  (frame time clamped to between 0 and 0.1, or the fixed step under  fixedstep ). multiply speeds by it. a second argument is the same  input  that  useinput  gets:  useupdate((dt, input) => step(input, dt)) . \n      useinput  or  useupdate ?  something that happens  once, on a press  (open the menu, pause, fire) goes in  useinput . something that happens  over time  (move while a key is held, step the rules) goes in  useupdate((dt, input) => \u2026) , which reads the keys itself. both get the same input in the same step, so do not copy keys into a field to carry them from one hook to the other.  \n  \nuseupdate((dt) => {\n  s.x = clamp(s.x + speed * dt, 0, 600);\n});\n  \n   each handler hook may appear several times; they run in source order. \n\n    usemount  /  useunmount  \n  \nfunction banner({ text, ondone }: bannerprops) {\n  const s = usemutable({ shown: false });\n  usemount(() => { s.shown = true; }); // may write state: renders in the same frame\n  useunmount(() => ondone?.());        // may not write state (tse1402); tell the parent instead\n  return  ;\n}\n  \n   \n     mounts when attached to the world (a condition turns on, a list item is added); unmounts when detached. \n     each takes one arrow function with no parameters. \n     no  useeffect  (see  below ), but  usemount  may return a cleanup, as an effect does in react. \n   \n   a cleanup from  usemount  \n  \n// the call's result is the cleanup: settings.subscribe returns the function that stops it.\nusemount(() => settings.subscribe(() => { s.volume = settings.get().music; }));\n\n// or return one: an arrow, or a local holding a function.\nusemount(() => {\n  const stop = controls.subscribe(() => { s.keys = controls.tojson(); });\n  return () => {\n    stop();\n  };\n});\n  \n   \n     a function returned from  usemount  runs once, when the component leaves the world, after its  useunmount  handlers. anything else returned is ignored. an expression body's call is returned, as in react ( () => listen(\u2026) ). \n     in any handler, an arrow may be handed to a plain function, a method of an imported object, a callback prop or a local ( settings.subscribe(() => \u2026) ). it is a handler of its own: it may write state and call actions. outside a handler, an arrow cannot be handed on. \n     a hot reload removes the old tree, so its cleanups run and the new tree subscribes again. \n   \n\n   no  useeffect : what to write instead \n   there is no  useeffect , on purpose. here an effect would run a frame after the change it answers (and a frame more for each effect it sets off), and it would pull game logic out of the rules, which are plain functions you can test and replay without a window. each job an effect does in react is done another way here: \n    \n      you want  write  \n      a sound, a burst or a shake when something happens  do it where it happens: the rules return events, a game-state action answers them through effect stores ( sfx.play(\"hit\") ,  fx.burst(\u2026) ). see  effects .  \n      a value computed from state or props  a derived  const : worked out again when what it reads changes.  \n      music, an emitter or the camera to follow state  a built-in with props:  <music> ,  <particles emitting> ,  <camera bounds> .  \n      something when the component comes or goes   usemount  /  useunmount .  \n      to subscribe to something outside the game state  subscribe in  usemount  and return what stops it:  usemount(() => settings.subscribe(() => \u2026)) . see  the cleanup .  \n      to start a component's state over when what it shows changes  a  key  on the element, as in react:  <dialogue key={talk.id} /> . see  keyed elements .  \n    \n   writing  useeffect  is  tse1102 , and the message points here. the reasons in full are in  docs/design.md , \u201cwhy there is no  useeffect \u201d. \n\n    usesound  \n  \nconst playhit = usesound(\"hit.wav\");   // path must be a string literal\nuseinput((input) => { if (input.pressed(\"space\")) playhit(); });\n  \n   call it as a statement in a handler (elsewhere is  tse1305 );  playhit(0.5)  plays at half volume. a sound played again while sounding overlaps itself, up to four at once. paths are relative to  <game assets> . sounds preload. \n\n   what handler code may contain \n   handlers (hook bodies and callback props) may contain: \n   \n      statements  \n        if  /  else , early  return  \n        const  locals \n       writing a field:  s.x = 5 ,  s.x += 1 ,  s.x++  (and  %= ,  **= ,  ??= ,  ||= ,  &&= ) \n        let  locals, optionally typed ( let best: enemy | null = null ), assigned the same ways \n        for (const e of enemies) { \u2026 } , destructuring too:  for (const [i, { id }] of enemies.entries())  \n        for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) ,  while  and  do\u2026while , all with  break  and  continue  \n        try  /  catch  /  finally  and  throw  \n        switch  (falling through as in js) and  { \u2026 }  blocks \n       calling a sound function or a  usegame  action ( const events = step(input, dt)  may use its result) \n       calling a plain function, or a method of an imported object ( fx.burst(\u2026) ) \n       calling a callback prop:  ondone?.() , or a local holding a function:  stop()  \n       handing an arrow to any of those:  settings.subscribe(() => { s.view = read(); })  \n       in  usemount  only, returning a cleanup:  return () => stop();  \n      \n      calls and members  \n        math.abs floor ceil round sign sqrt sin cos min max  \n       the array, string and number methods allowed in a component body ( id.startswith(\"bind:\") ,  id.slice(5) ) \n        clamp(value, min, max)  (3 args) \n        freeze(steps) : skip every  useupdate  for that many steps \n        input.pressed / down / released ,  input.pointerx / pointery  \n       a value the handler made itself (an array or object literal,  new , or what a call returns): any method ( list.push(e) ,  seen.add(id) ,  best.sort((a, b) => a.hp - b.hp) ) and assignment into it ( tally.hits++ ,  grid[y]![x] = 1 ). a local that only reads state or a prop ( const list = enemies ,  enemies.find(\u2026) ) is not the handler's: state is changed with its setter. \n        new  of  map ,  set ,  weakmap ,  weakset ,  array , the  error  types, or a class imported from a  .ts  file (not  date : it reads the clock) \n       the standard library as written:  object.* ,  json.* ,  number.* ,  array.* ,  string.* , any  math  function,  console.* ,  parseint ,  parsefloat ,  isnan ,  isfinite .  math.random()  warns: replays and tests will not repeat it. \n        typeof ,  instanceof  and  in , which also work in a component body \n        other.tag ,  other.owner  in collision callbacks \n       template literals \n      \n   \n   a function's parameters are plain names, typed or not. one may be optional,  (step?: number) => \u2026 , and stays optional in the generated function; a default ( step = 1 ) or a rest parameter is not taken, so give the default in the body:  step ?? 1 . \n   a component has no  as : a cast overrides the type-checker, so it goes in a function in a  .ts  module, where it is easy to find and to test, and the component calls that.  table[key]!  is kept as written and  value satisfies point  keeps the value alone. (the one  as  a component writes is the type of a  usemutable  field,  spawns: [] as spawn[] .) \n   anything else ( var , a labelled  break , assigning to anything but a  usemutable  field or a  let ) is  tse1301 ,  tse1401  or  tse1402 . \n "},{"id":"logic","title":"Custom hooks and plain functions","page":"language.html","group":"Language","text":" \n   custom hooks and plain functions \n   logic you would extract in react, you extract here: into a custom hook (state and handlers, reused) or a plain function in a  .ts  file (a calculation, a table). \n\n   custom hooks \n   a function named  use\u2026  whose body is what a component body may hold is a  custom hook . a call to it as a statement of a component body is replaced by the hook's own statements before compiling, so every rule still applies. \n  \nfunction useplatformerbody() {\n  const step = usegame((s) => s.step);\n  useupdate((dt, input) => { step(input.down(\"right\") ? 1 : 0, dt); });\n}\n\nexport function player() {\n  useplatformerbody();               // expands in place\n  return  ;\n}\n  \n   \n     each call gets its own state, so two calls make two separate states. \n     an argument that is a literal or an import is written into the hook (so it can be a  usesound  path, or a store the hook subscribes to); any other argument is bound to a  const , so it is worked out once. defaults work, and so does a destructured parameter,  function usemover({ speed, limit }: options) . \n     a hook may take a function and call it in a handler:  useevery(1, () => { s.ticks += 1; }) . the arrow becomes a function of the component (see below). \n     a caller takes what a hook returns as  const v = usex() ,  const {a, b} = usex()  (object literal),  const [a, b] = usex()  (array literal). a name the hook returns as it is takes the caller's name, so a hook can return its  usemutable  ( return s; ) and the caller reads and writes its fields. \n     a hook is inlined, not called: each call's statements are written into the component. that is why each call has its own state, and why a hook cannot be called in a condition or a loop (the same rule as react, for another reason), nor call itself. \n     a hook is declared in the same file or imported from a relative  .tsx . hooks may call hooks, up to six deep. \n     a call anywhere but a statement of the component body is  tse1101 . \n   \n\n   hooks the engine ships \n   these are ordinary custom hooks, imported from  @tsengine/runtime/jsx . \n  \nconst squash = usesquash();                        // springs back to 1 at 1.75 a second\nsquash.x = 0.6;                                    // in a handler: thin and tall\nsquash.y = 1.4;\n \n\nconst shake = useshake();\nshake.strength = 3;                                // in a handler: starts a shake\nshake.duration = 0.25;\nshake.elapsed = 0;\n   \n\nconst time = usetime();                            // seconds since it mounted\n\nconst values = usestore(settings, settingsview);   // a store outside the game state, kept up to date\n\nconst device = useinputdevice();                   // \"keyboard\", \"pointer\" or \"gamepad\": what was pressed last\n\nconst window = usewindowsize();                    // { width, height } of the window, kept up to date\n\nconst { x, y, move, touching } = usebody({ x: 16, y: 32, width: 8, height: 11 });   // moves, stopped by what is solid\n\nconst { x, y, contacts } = useball({ x: 40, y: 0, radius: 6, gravity: 600 });      // falls and piles up with other balls\n  \n    usestore(store, read)  takes anything with  subscribe(listener)  that returns a stop function ( createsettings  stores,  controls , a store of your own) and a function of no arguments that returns what to show, both imported from  .ts  files. it renders only when the data  read  returns differs ( keepifequal , which a handler can call too). \n    useinputdevice()  says what the player is using, so a prompt can be worded for it:  device === \"keyboard\" ? \"press enter\" : \"tap to start\" . a mouse and a finger are both  \"pointer\" : a touch is a left click to a game. it is  \"pointer\"  until something is pressed, so a game on a phone never names a key, and the component renders only when it changes. outside a component,  getinputdevice()  reads the same value. \n    usewindowsize()  is the window's size in logical pixels (a phone's points), so a landscape game can ask a phone held upright to be turned:  window.height > window.width && <rotateprompt /> . it is the window, not the game's resolution: a  <screen>  is still scaled into it as its  fit  says. the component renders only when the size changes. outside a component,  getwindowsize()  reads the same value, and a test can set it with  setwindowsize(width, height) . \n\n   plain functions and constants \n   import functions and constants from a relative  .ts  file and use them in any expression: a prop, a derived value, a handler. you can also pass a function as a value. the compiler does not look inside: typescript checks the call, and the function is assumed pure. loops, arrays and anything else are fine in its body. \n  \nimport { stepplayer, spawnin } from \"./rules/player.ts\";\nimport { playevents } from \"./effects.ts\";\nimport { room_list } from \"./rules/rooms.ts\";\n\nexport function player() {\n  const { room } = usegame((s) => s.run);\n  const s = usemutable({ me: spawnin(0) });            // a per-instance object from a plain function\n\n  useupdate((dt, input) => {\n    const step = stepplayer(s.me, room, readcontrols(input), dt); // plain typescript: loops, tables, anything\n    s.me = step.next;\n    playevents(step.events);                                       // sounds, bursts, freeze\n  });\n  \u2026\n}\n\n{room_list.filter((r) => math.abs(r.index - room)    )}\n  \n   this is how kite, the platformer in  games/kite , is built, and why: a solver proves each room can be finished by stepping the character thousands of times from copied states, which plain functions make cheap. for anything else, keep the game in components (hearth's enemies and towers), which   @tsengine/testing   tests as easily as a function. \n   \n     the name must not start with  use . \n     a dynamic prop that calls a function re-evaluates when the state in its arguments changes. \n     a handler may keep the result of a plain function or a  usegame  action in a  const  and use it. in jsx or a derived value, a call would run on every render. \n     nothing re-renders when code changes an imported constant. that suits data and effect stores, not game state (use  usegame ). \n     you can call methods of imported objects ( fx.burst(\u2026) ) in handlers and expressions. an array method on an imported array ( rooms.filter ) is still lowered, so its callback may read state. \n   \n "},{"id":"transform","title":"Transform and drawing order","page":"language.html","group":"Language","text":" \n   position and drawing order \n   every built-in except  game ,  screen ,  router ,  route ,  fade  and  music  takes these props. all are optional and may come from state. \n  \n \n \n  \n    \n      prop  default  meaning  \n       x ,  y   0  pixels, relative to the parent.  y points down.   \n       rotation   0  radians. positive turns clockwise.  \n       scalex ,  scaley   1  per-axis scale. negative flips.  \n       z   0  draw order: higher is drawn later, so on top. negative is behind. it adds to the parent's, so raising a component raises everything in it.  \n       global   false  true ( global  alone, or  global={pinned} ): the element is placed in world space and ignores the parent.  \n        \n   opacity is a prop on every element too:  <entity opacity={fade}> ,  <shape opacity={0.5} \u2026 /> ,  <sprite opacity={\u2026} \u2026 /> . it is 0 to 1 and fades the element and everything inside it, so an  <entity>  fades a whole popup or card at once. it multiplies down the tree (0.5 inside 0.5 is 0.25), and each thing is drawn at it on its own, so where two overlap the one below shows through. to fade a component, wrap it:  <entity opacity={\u2026}><card /></entity> ; on the component itself  opacity  is whatever its own props say. \n   \n     the origin is the top-left corner of a  sprite ,  animatedsprite ,  shape ,  text ,  collider ,  pointer ; rotation and scale are about it. \n     children are relative:  x: 10  inside a parent at  x: 100  is at 110, and turns and scales with the parent. \n     equal  z  keeps tree order, globally across the tree. \n     a component takes none of these: on a component,  x  is whatever its props say. place one by wrapping it,  <entity x={40} y={8}><coin /></entity> , or give it  x  and  y  props and put them on its root, as hearth's  pad  does. \n     there is no  transform  attribute:  transform={{ \u2026 }}  is  tse1207 , whose message says what to write. \n   \n "},{"id":"props","title":"Props, callbacks and children","page":"language.html","group":"Language","text":" \n   props, callbacks and children \n   props come through a destructured parameter typed with a props type written in the parameter ( ({ hp }: { hp: number }) , which is then called  heartprops  for a  heart ) or declared in the same file ( ({ hp }: heartprops) ). a changed value updates the child in place; it is never recreated. \n  \ninterface enemyprops {\n  speed: number;\n  tint?: \"red\" | \"blue\";          // optional; literal unions become scene-settable choices\n  ondefeated?: () => void;        // callback prop\n}\n\nexport function enemy({ speed, tint = \"red\", ondefeated }: enemyprops) {\n  const s = usemutable({ hp: 3 });\n  useupdate(() => { if (s.hp    ;\n}\n\n// parent\n \n    10 ? 2 : 1} ondefeated={() => { s.score += 1; }} />\n \n  \n   \n      props: enemyprops  also works ( props.speed ), but  props  cannot be passed on or spread. defaults go in the destructuring. \n     a prop may be read under another name, as in react:  ({ seed: firstseed, speed: rate = 2 }: enemyprops) , so a prop and the state it seeds can both be called what they are. \n     callback props carry messages from child to parent. call optional ones with  ?.() . whether one was passed may be tested:  onadjust === undefined , or by its truth in a choice (below). \n     a callback attribute may be a choice:  onpress={open ? close : undefined} . each branch is an arrow, a function passed whole (a callback prop, a function of the component's, an action) or  undefined , and the choice is made again when what its condition reads changes. while the branch taken is  undefined  a built-in has no handler, so the box does not take the press or the mouse; the same goes for  <flex onpress={onpress}>  when  onpress  was not passed. a branch whose test proves a callback prop was passed may call it:  onpress === undefined ? undefined : () => onpress() . to a component, each branch is passed whole: declare the arrow in the body ( const go = (n: number) => \u2026 ) and write  ongo={open ? go : undefined} . \n     a bad props form is  tse1208 . \n   \n\n   passing children \n   declare  children: child  and place  {children}  once, directly inside a container (the root, a fragment, or a static  <entity> ). the  parent  owns what it passes and updates it from its own state. \n  \nimport { type child, entity, sprite } from \"@tsengine/runtime/jsx\";\n\ninterface panelprops { title: string; children: child }\n\nexport function panel({ title, children }: panelprops) {\n  return (\n     \n       \n       {children} \n     \n  );\n}\n\n// usage: the children follow `score` without panel knowing\n \n   \n  {score > 10 &&  }\n \n  \n   a component can build on another and pass its  {children}  on, between children of its own; and what a parent passes may mix single elements, conditions and a list. each keeps the place it was written in, as items come, go and move. \n  \n// card is a panel with a title bar and a footer: what card is given goes between them.\nexport function card({ title, children }: panelprops) {\n  return (\n     \n       {title} \n      {children}\n       press z \n     \n  );\n}\n\n// usage: a heading, then one row per item\n \n   items \n  {s.items.map((item) =>  )}\n \n  \n   text wrappers \n   a component that writes text in one style takes its words as children, as  <text>  does: declare  children: textchild | readonly textchild[]  and pass them on with  <text>{children}</text> . \n  \nimport { text, type textchild } from \"@tsengine/runtime/jsx\";\n\ninterface labelprops { color?: string; children: textchild | readonly textchild[] }\n\nexport function label({ color = \"#fff1d6\", children }: labelprops) {\n  return  {children} ;\n}\n\n// usage: words and {values}, as in  \n coins: {coins} \n game over \n  \n   \n     the words become one string, passed as the  children  prop: a change of  coins  renders the text again, nothing else. \n     a text wrapper may pass its words on to another ( <button>  holding a  <label>{children}</label> ). \n     a component takes text or elements, never both at once:  children: child  is for elements. \n     when the whole game writes in one size and colour, set them once at the root instead:  <game textsize={10} textcolor=\"#e8d9a8\"> . every  <text>  without its own  size  or  color  uses them. they are not inherited from containers: a text's look never depends on where it is in the tree. \n   \n "},{"id":"control","title":"Conditions and lists","page":"language.html","group":"Language","text":" \n   conditions and lists \n   jsx children are elements, conditionals or keyed lists. anything else (text, other expressions) is  tse1202 , except text inside  <text>  and inside a  text wrapper . \n   a list's callback takes the item and, if wanted, its index:  (item, index) => . the item may be taken apart into plain members,  ({ id, x }) =>  or  ({ id: n }) => , with no default, rest or nested pattern. \n   a list can sit under a condition:  {show && items.map((item) => <row key={item.id} />)} . \n   conditionals \n  \n{health  }\n{taken ? null :  }\n{open ?   :  }          // either branch may be null\n{gate !== undefined &&  }\n  \n   a condition turning off unmounts the child; turning on mounts it. to switch several siblings on one condition, wrap them in a fragment:  {gems === 0 && (<><shape \u2026 /><collider \u2026 /></>)} . \n\n   keyed lists \n  \n{enemies.map((e) => (\n    remove(e.id)} />\n))}\n\n// the items can be computed where they are drawn, as in react: a member, a mapped array, a helper's result.\n{spotsof(run).map((s) =>  )}\n\n// an item may hold children, nested and conditional, that read it.\n{slots.map((slot) => (\n   \n     \n    {slot.filled &&  {slot.name} }\n   \n))}\n\n// and a list of its own, which may read the item: a grid of rows of cells.\n{rows.map((row) => (\n   \n    {row.cells.map((cell) =>   toggle(row.id, cell.id)} />)}\n   \n))}\n  \n    \n      rule (otherwise  tse1209 )  \n      the items are any array the component can compute: state or a prop, a member of one ( world.enemies ), an array method's result ( .filter ,  .map ,  .slice ), an imported constant, or what a plain function returns ( spotsof(run) ). the list follows the state and props the expression reads. not through  ?. .  \n       map  takes one inline arrow (item, optionally index) returning one element,  cond && <el key \u2026 />  or  cond ? <a key \u2026 /> : <b key \u2026 /> : an expression body, or a block with only  return . no fragments.  \n      the element may have children, nested and conditional ( {slot.filled && <text \u2026 />} ), that read the item, and lists of its own ( {row.cells.map(\u2026)} ), nested as deep as needed. they cannot include  {children}  or a  <route> ; for those, make the item a component.  \n      the element has a  key  that reads the item ( key={e.id} ). a key reading only the index compiles with a warning; one reading neither is an error.  \n      a callback on the item may read the item but not the index; one in a nested list may read the items around it too.  \n    \n   instances follow keys, so each keeps its own state as items move. a computed list is worked out once per render of the component, which happens when what it reads changes. \n\n   keyed elements \n   a  key  on an element outside a list starts it over when the key changes, as in react: the old instance unmounts and a new one mounts, with fresh state. while the key stays the same, a changed prop updates it in place, as usual. \n  \n         // a new conversation starts at its first page\n{open &&  }  // a keyed branch of a condition works too\n  \n   \n     the key is any value that identifies what the element shows (a string or a number), and may read state and props. \n     the new instance is added before the old one is removed, as with list items, so its  usemount  runs before the old one's  useunmount . \n     not on a component's root ( tse1203 ): put the key where the component is used. not inside a list item's children ( tse1209 ): make the item a component. beside other children passed to a component it is fine. \n   \n "},{"id":"gamestate","title":"Game state (useGame)","page":"language.html","group":"Language","text":" \n   game state:  creategamestate  \n   the  game state  is what every component shares (score, hit points): its data and the actions that change it. a component reads with a selector and calls actions; only actions change it. \n  \n// gamestate.ts, in the source directory\nimport { creategamestate } from \"@tsengine/runtime\";\n\nexport const usegame = creategamestate(\n  { stats: { coins: 0, hits: 0 } },                   // the data: a plain object\n  {\n    collect(s) { s.stats.coins += 1; },               // an action takes the state and writes it directly\n    hurt(s, n: number) { s.stats.hits += n; },        // then its own arguments: hurt(2)\n  },\n);\n  \n  \nimport { usegame } from \"./gamestate.ts\";\n\nfunction coincounter() {\n  const coins = usegame((s) => s.stats.coins);       // read: re-renders when this leaf changes\n  return  coins: {coins} ;\n}\n\nfunction player() {\n  const { collect } = usegame.actions();              // the actions it calls: subscribes to nothing\n  // ...oncollisionenter={() => collect()}\n}\n  \n   rules \n   \n     a selector is a path ( s.stats.coins ) to a leaf, an object or an array, or any expression of paths ( (s) => s.cursor === id ,  (s) => focusof(s.battle, s.cursor) ): each path it reads is subscribed to, and the value is worked out again when one changes. a typo is  tse1108 . call  usegame(...)  at the top level, bound to a  const , or destructured:  const { room, deaths } = usegame((s) => s.run) . \n     actions come from  const { collect, hurt } = usegame.actions() . they never change, so this subscribes to nothing. a name there that is a value of the state, or a typo, is  tse1108 ; taking actions from the whole state ( usegame((s) => s) ) still works but warns. in plain code,  usegame.actions().collect() . \n     an action may be passed whole:  onpress={resume} . \n     call an action as a statement in a handler, not in jsx or a derived value. \n     an action writes the state directly, nested objects and lists too ( s.run.time += dt ,  s.gems.push(id) ). it writes a draft; when it returns, only the values that changed are committed, and only their readers hear of it. an action called from another shares its draft. one that throws changes nothing. \n     only components reading something that  changed  re-render. every game starts from the initial state. \n     the data must be an object literal: number, string and boolean literals, nested objects, and other values (arrays,  newrun() ) kept as values; no functions. the actions are an object literal of functions. spreads, computed keys and a name in both are  tse1109 . \n     what a selector returns is read-only, deep:  run.time = 0  or  items.push(x)  in a component is  tse1402  /  tse1301 , whose message names the action to add, and a helper that takes a mutable  item[]  is a type error for a selected list (take  readonly item[] ). \n     an action may call another ( usegame.actions().begin() ), and plain code it calls (a save's  pick ) reads what it has written so far through  usegame.getstate() . \n      usegame.getstate()  reads the state outside components.  usegame.setstate({ \u2026 })  replaces part of it (shallow-merged) for tests, a story's  setup  and tools; game code uses actions, and  setstate  while an action runs is an error. \n     the zustand form,  creategamestate<state>()((set) => ({ \u2026 })) , is not supported:  tse1109  says what to write instead. \n   \n   kite keeps  screen: \"title\" | \"play\" | \"end\"  in the game state and gives it to  <router route={screen}> , so any action ( begin ,  finished ) changes screens. actions are plain typescript: they can call a sound store ( sfx.play(\"start\") ) as well as write the state. \n     usemutable, context or usegame?   usemutable  for what one component owns; context for what a component shares with everything below it;  usegame  for what the whole game shares.  \n\n   sharing state between components \n   as in react, what two components need lives in their nearest common parent, so you lift it up. the parent keeps it in  usemutable , a child reports to it through a callback prop, and the parent hands it down as a prop, or through context to a deep subtree. the game state is the top of that ladder, for what the whole game shares (the screen, the score). \n  \nexport function level() {\n  const s = usemutable({ playerx: 0 });\n  return (\n     \n      {/* reports where it is */}\n        { s.playerx = x; }} />\n      {/* by props */}\n       \n      {/* or through context, to every enemy below */}\n       \n         \n       \n     \n  );\n}\n  \n   a handler reads a prop or a context value as the parent has it now: an enemy after the player in the tree sees where the player moved  this  step, one before it sees where it was when the step began. the order is the tree's, parents first, the same on every run (see  how a frame works ). render shows what the step committed. \n\n   context \n   context works as in react: a component provides a value to everything below it, and a component reads it where it is needed instead of having it passed down through every level. \n  \n// theme.ts\nimport { createcontext } from \"@tsengine/runtime\";\nexport const theme = createcontext({ accent: \"#ffb15e\", size: 10 });\n\n// a component: everything under the provider reads `palette`\n \n   \n \n\n// a component anywhere under it\nconst theme = usecontext(theme);              // or: const { accent } = usecontext(theme);\nreturn  \u2026 ;\n  \n   \n      usecontext  reads the nearest provider of that context above the component, or the context's default when there is none. the component renders again when that provider's  value  changes. \n     a provider is a container that layout sees through and that draws nothing. it is written  <theme.provider value>  and compiled into the built-in  <provider context={theme} value> . \n     the context is made once, in a  .ts  file, and imported. a component finds its provider when it joins the world; components are never moved, so it keeps it. \n   \n "},{"id":"collisions","title":"Collisions and pointer presses","page":"systems.html","group":"Systems","text":" \n   collisions and presses \n   there is no physics: the player notices when boxes start and stop touching and calls your callbacks. a  collider  is an invisible box with its top-left at the object's position. pair it with a  shape  to see it. \n  \n  {\n    if (other.tag === \"coin\") collect();\n    else if (other.owner === \"spikes\") s.hurt = true;\n  }}\n  oncollisionexit={(other) => { if (other.tag === \"spikes\") s.hurt = false; }}\n/>\n  \n    \n      concept  meaning  \n       other.tag   the other collider's  tag  ( \"\"  when none). tells apart colliders on one prefab.  \n       other.owner   the  name  of the nearest  <entity name>  at or above the other collider, or its component's class name. tells apart prefabs.  \n       other.data   what the other collider's  data  prop holds:  <collider tag=\"coin\" data={{ value }} />  lets the player read  other.data.value . typed by tag once the game declares  colliderdata  (below);  unknown  until then.  \n       layer  /  hits   a pair is tested only when each side's  hits  allows the other's  layer . use them to skip pairs cheaply (bullets ignore bullets). unrelated to  tag .  \n       oncollisionexit   fires once when a touching pair separates, or when the other leaves the world. it does not fire for a collider that itself left.  \n      in a  <hud>   colliders never touch anything.  \n    \n   shapes \n   a collider is a box unless it says otherwise.  shape=\"circle\"  is the circle inside its box (centred, half the shorter side across);  shape=\"polygon\"  with  points={[0, 16, 16, 16, 16, 0]}  is a convex polygon in its own pixels, inside its box (a ramp, a blade). pairs are found by their boxes first. a pair in which either collider has a shape is then tested exactly, rotated with its objects, so two round coins whose boxes meet at the corners do not touch. a pair of plain boxes is tested as upright boxes.  usebody 's  touching  and  overlapping  see a shape too. a body is always a box, and a  solid  collider stops bodies at its box whatever its shape, so there are no slopes. \n  \n \n \n  \n   on  other , handlers can read  tag ,  owner  and  data  and nothing else. to type  data , declare once, in any  .ts  file, what each tag carries; comparing  other.tag  then narrows  other.data : \n  \n// colliders.ts\ndeclare global {\n  interface colliderdata { coin: { value: number }; enemy: { damage: number }; player: undefined }\n}\nexport {};\n\n// in a component: if (other.tag === \"coin\") s.coins += other.data.value;\n  \n   once it is declared, every tag a game compares must be in it. \n     collider or tile?  contacts are found after every step, and whatever a callback changes is rendered before the next step and drawn in the same frame. a callback still runs  after  the step's  useupdate , though, so code that must know mid-step (movement against walls, a precise death) reads tiles instead: kite reads spikes and exits as tiles inside its movement step, and uses  collider  for the optional gem pickup.  \n   finding and hitting things in an area:  nearby  and  send  \n   a component has no handle on another, so a tower cannot call an enemy. it asks the colliders instead.  nearby(tag, x, y, radius)  lists the colliders with that tag whose boxes come within a circle (a real circle, not a square), nearest first. each entry has the collider's  tag ,  owner  and  data , the middle of its box ( x ,  y ) and its  distance  (0 inside the box).  send(tag, where, value)  hands  value  to the  onreceive  of every collider with that tag in a circle ( { x, y, radius } ), or to the single collider a  nearby  result describes. call both from a handler ( useupdate ,  useinput , a callback prop). neither includes the colliders that the calling component draws itself, so a send never reaches its sender. a move that should also affect its user (a buff on everyone in range, the caster included) applies that part to the caster directly. \n  \n// a tower: the nearest enemy in reach, hit; or everything in a splash.\nuseupdate(() => {\n  const foes = nearby(\"enemy\", x, y, reach);\n  if (foes.length === 0) return;\n  send(\"enemy\", foes[0], { damage: 5, from: id });                          // that one enemy\n  send(\"enemy\", { x: foes[0].x, y: foes[0].y, radius: 16 }, { damage: 2, from: id }); // every enemy in the splash\n});\n\n// an enemy: what it was sent, typed by its tag.\n  { s.hp -= blast.damage; }} />\n  \n    \n      rule  meaning  \n      when it arrives  at the end of the step's update phase: after every  useupdate  and timer, before the step renders and before its collisions are found. so it lands in the step it was sent, and what a receiver changes is drawn that step.  \n      in what order  the order values were sent (handlers run in tree order, so that order repeats), each send's receivers nearest first. a value sent from an  onreceive  is delivered later in the same phase; one sent from a collision callback, at the end of the next step's update phase. replays and the two players give the same result.  \n      where things are  boxes are where they were when the step's updates began, as for  usebody . a  nearby  result is good for its step: keep an id from its  data , not the result, across steps. a receiver that left the world before delivery hears nothing.  \n      types  declare what each tag can be sent beside  colliderdata :  interface collidermessages { enemy: blast } . then  send(\"enemy\", \u2026, value)  and  onreceive 's value are typed by the tag; undeclared, the value is  unknown .  \n    \n   use  send  in place of a collider that lives for one step only to carry a message (a blast's collider whose  data  every enemy it touches checks). there is no extra collider and no step of delay, and the receiver does not have to check whether the blast was meant for it. \n   pointer presses \n   a left click goes to the topmost  pointer  or  flex onpress  under the mouse. hud buttons work however the camera moves. \n "},{"id":"placing","title":"Placing things: Flex, x and y","page":"systems.html","group":"Systems","text":" \n   placing things \n   anything laid out in rows or columns is a  <flex> , wherever it is (a menu over the world, a row of hearts, an inventory, a panel in a  <hud> ). anything the game moves (a character, a coin, a shot) is placed with  x  and  y  from its state. \n    x ,  y ,  z ,  rotation ,  scalex ,  scaley ,  global  and  opacity  are props of every built-in that can be placed:  <sprite image=\"coin.png\" x={spot.x} y={spot.y} /> . a component takes none of them, since a component may declare an  x  of its own. to place one, wrap it in an  <entity> ,  <entity x={40} y={8}><coin /></entity> , or give it  x  and  y  props that it puts on its root (see  position and drawing order ). \n   bramble's menus sit in a  <screen> , outside any  <hud> . here is one with its rows placed by hand: \n  \n// before: every row placed by hand, and the bar behind the cursor's row worked out the same way.\n \n   \n  {rows.map((row, i) => (\n     \n       {row.label} \n       {row.value} \n     \n  ))}\n \n  \n   as a column, the layout places the rows and the bar, which becomes the row's  background . adding a row or lengthening a label needs no position changed by hand, and the menu draws exactly the same pixels. \n  \n// games/bramble/src/menu.tsx\n \n  {rows.map((row, i) => (\n     \n       {row.label} \n       {row.value} \n     \n  ))}\n \n  \n   rows and columns:  flex  \n   a  <flex>  lays out its children in a row or column, with flexbox props and clay-style sizing. a child's own  x  and  y  still offset it within its slot. only a  flex  is resized;  sprite ,  shape  and  text  keep their size. \n    \n      size value  meaning  \n       \"fit\"  (default)  the children plus padding and gaps.  \n       \"grow\"   the fit size plus a share of the parent's free space.  \n       120   fixed pixels.  \n       \"50%\"   a percentage of the parent's inner size.  \n    \n  \n \n   \n     \n     {coins} \n    {/* a spacer */}\n     \n   \n  {/* a button */}\n    { s.paused = !s.paused; }}>\n     {s.paused ? \"resume\" : \"pause\"} \n   \n \n  \n   a  flex  can be styled with props, which are checked like the layout ones:  background ,  borderwidth  and  bordercolor  (the border is drawn inside the edge and takes room like padding), and  borderradius .  hoverbackground  and  hoverbordercolor  are drawn while the mouse is over the box, and  focusbackground  and  focusbordercolor  while it has the focus; hover wins when both apply.  pressedbackground  and  pressedbordercolor  are drawn while the mouse button is held on the box, and  disabled  (with  disabledbackground  and  disabledbordercolor ) switches a button off: no press, key or focus reaches it, and it shows only its disabled colours. over one another the states go plain, focus, hover, pressed, and disabled over all. they change colours only, so nothing moves when the mouse does, and the component does not render again. give the box a  transition  (seconds) and its colours fade between states instead of snapping; a fade that is cut short reverses from wherever it had reached. a box with a hover prop takes the mouse but lets a press through to what is under it, unless it has an  onpress . \n  \n \n   start \n \n  \n    <slider>  and  <toggle>  are two controls that are  flex  boxes with parts drawn in them, so they are sized, laid out, clipped, faded, disabled and styled like any other. a slider is  <slider value={s.volume} min={0} max={10} step={1} onchange={(v) => { s.volume = v; }} /> : a press puts the thumb under the mouse, a drag moves it (even once the mouse has left the box), and when it has the focus the left and right keys move it one step (a twentieth of the range with no  step ) while up and down move the focus on. the track is its  background , so it takes a border, a radius, an image and a colour for each state;  fillcolor  is the part left of the thumb. a toggle is  <toggle checked={s.sound} onchange={(on) => { s.sound = on; }} /> : a press, the confirm key or its  presskey  flips it. each moves its own value and then calls  onchange  with the new one, only when it is different, so  value  and  checked  give the starting value and any value you set later. both take the focus by default. \n  \n \n    { s.volume = v; }} />\n    { s.sound = on; }} />\n \n  \n    <button>  and  <menu>  are the two pieces most screens need. a button is a  flex  that takes the focus and has a look for each state, its label as children; any  flex  prop replaces the default look. a menu is a column whose focusable children are its items: while one of them has the focus, up and down move it only among them, wrapping round at the ends ( loop={false}  stops there), confirm presses the focused one, and  back  (escape or padb without a  back  action) calls  onback .  autofocus  says which item starts with the focus.  onadjust  on any focusable box hears left and right while it has the focus (-1 or 1), for a row that changes a value in place; the focus stays. when a modal opens over a menu its focus is put aside, and given back when the modal closes. a component of your own called  button  or  menu  is still yours: a name declared or imported in a file shadows the built-in, unless the file imports the built-in too. \n  \n \n    resume  \n    { s.volume += 1; }} onadjust={(d) => { s.volume += d; }}>\n     volume {s.volume} \n   \n    quit  \n \n  \n    wrap  lets a row break into lines: a child that will not fit in what is left of a line starts the next one. give the box a width ( width={120} ,  \"50%\"  or  \"grow\" ); with only a fit size it is one line. lines stack downward, each as tall as its tallest child, with  rowgap  between lines and  columngap  between the children of a line (both default to  gap ).  justifycontent  places the children of each line on its own, so a short last line is not spread like a full one, and  alignitems  places each child inside its line's height. children keep their size (a  \"grow\"  child takes only its content's size, and none shrink); one wider than the box is alone on a line and sticks out. only rows wrap. to scroll a long grid, put it in a  direction=\"column\"  scroll box with  width=\"grow\"  on the grid, so its width is the box's and it wraps there instead of running off to the side. \n  \n \n   \n    {items.map((item) =>  )}\n   \n \n  \n    opacity  fades a box and everything in it, from 1 (as it is) to 0 (gone):  <flex opacity={open ? 1 : 0} transition={0.2}>  is a panel that fades in and out. it multiplies into every colour, sprite and piece of text under the box, so a faded box inside a faded box is at the product of the two. each part is drawn at that opacity by itself and not flattened into one picture first, so where parts overlap (a dark button on a panel) one shows through the other while the box is part way, and a text  outline , whose copies overlap, fades more slowly than the rest. at 0 nothing is drawn, but the box is still laid out and still takes the mouse; switch it  disabled  or take it out of the tree if it should not. \n  \n \n   settings \n \n  \n    backgroundimage  draws a picture behind the children, over the  background  colour, cut into nine by  slice : the four corners keep their size, the four edges stretch along one side and the middle along both, so one small image skins a panel or a button at any size. the four sides can differ ( slicetop ,  sliceright ,  slicebottom ,  sliceleft ).  hoverbackgroundimage ,  pressedbackgroundimage ,  focusbackgroundimage  and  disabledbackgroundimage  swap the picture by state like the colours do. edges and middle are stretched, not tiled, and with no smoothing the pixels stay crisp; a box smaller than its corners shrinks them together. the name is checked against the assets folder when you build. \n  \n \n   start \n \n  \n    overflow  says what happens to children that stick out of a box:  \"visible\"  (the default) draws them outside it,  \"hidden\"  clips them to it, and  \"scroll\"  clips them and lets the mouse wheel or a drag move them. a scroll box needs a size smaller than its content (a fixed  height , or a  maxheight ); its children keep their size instead of shrinking to fit. a notch of the wheel moves it 32 pixels, the box under the mouse takes it, and one at its end hands it to the scroll box around it. a drag scrolls it as a finger scrolls a page: once the pointer has moved 6 pixels from where it was pressed, the content follows it. a touch is the left mouse button, so a mouse drags it too. over a box with something to scroll, a press is heard when the button is let go, and not at all if the drag scrolled: a drag on a row of a list, or on a card whose text scrolls, scrolls without pressing it. everywhere else a press is heard as it goes down. a drag that begins on a box that takes drags itself, such as a  <slider>  or one with  ondrag , is that box's and does not scroll.  scrollx  and  scrolly  are where the content starts and what the wheel changes; set the prop to jump there (clamped to the content, so  scrolly={9999}  keeps a chat log at its end). a child cut off by a clip cannot be pressed or hovered where it is cut off. a clip stops at the inside of the box's border, so content never paints over it, and is the rectangle around the box, so one that is rotated is clipped to the rectangle that holds it. a scroll box shows a thin bar along each axis that overflows, as long as the box is to its content as the bar is to the box ( scrollbar={false}  hides it;  scrollbarcolor  and  scrollbarwidth  style it). when the focus moves to something inside a scroll box, such as the next item of a long  <menu> , the box scrolls just enough to show it. \n  \n \n  {messages.map((m) =>  {m.text} )}\n \n  \n    float  takes a box out of the flow and attaches it to a point of its parent ( anchor ,  to ,  offsetx ,  offsety ): tooltips, badges. use  z  to draw it on top. \n    left ,  right ,  top  and  bottom  pin a box to its parent's edges, as they do an absolutely placed box in css: each is the gap between that edge of the parent and the same edge of the box, and giving any of them takes the box out of the flow.  <flex right={16} bottom={8}>  sits in the parent's bottom right corner whatever size the parent is. with both edges of an axis and no size on it, the box stretches between them ( left={0} right={0}  is a bar across). an axis with neither is placed by  anchor ,  to  and the offset. to pin a  <text>  or a  <sprite> , put it in a pinned  <flex> . a pinned box whose parent is not a  <flex>  is pinned to the game's area, so  <flex right={12} top={12}>  at the top of a screen is a panel in its corner. \n    <tooltip>  is a floating box that shows only while the mouse is over the box it is in, or that box has the focus. put it among the box's children; it sits above its parent by default ( anchor  and  to  move it), draws over what is near it, and takes no space. its parent takes the mouse for it, so a plain  <flex>  gets one too.  show  forces it on or off. \n  \n \n   sell \n    sells for {price} gold  \n \n  \n    <textinput>  is a one-line text field. give it the focus (a click, or the arrows) and type: the characters the keyboard makes go in at the end, with the system's key repeat, backspace takes the last one off, and enter calls  onsubmit . keep the text in state with  value  and  onchange , as with a react input; what  onchange  writes back is what the field shows, so it can change or refuse what is typed. while a field has the focus, letters do not trigger the game's  confirm  and  back  actions or a  presskey : only the arrows, enter and escape move or leave it. a game's own  useinput  still sees the keys; check  input.typed , the characters typed this step, if it needs to. \n  \n  { s.name = v; }} onsubmit={() => start(s.name)} />\n  \n\n "},{"id":"ui","title":"UI: Hud, Flex, Screen, Camera","page":"systems.html","group":"Systems","text":" \n   screen, hud and camera \n   the shape of a game root \n  \nexport default function main() {\n  return (\n     \n       \n        {/* your world: ground, coin, \u2026 */}\n         \n        {/* stays put while the camera moves */}\n         \n           \n             \n           \n         \n        {/* the view follows its children */}\n           \n       \n     \n  );\n}\n  \n    screen : fixed resolution \n    \n       fit   behaviour  \n       \"integer\"  (default)  largest whole-number scale that fits. every game pixel is the same size. bars fill the rest with  border .  \n       \"contain\"   largest scale that fits, fractional allowed.  \n       \"stretch\"   each axis scaled on its own to fill the window.  \n       \"expand\"   like  \"integer\" , but the game is widened to the window's shape. a wide window shows more world instead of bars. lay out with  grow  and percentages, not fixed x positions.  \n    \n   only the root  <screen>  counts; it takes no  x  or  y . everything inside is in game pixels. without one, coordinates are window pixels. \n\n    <text>  can keep itself readable over a busy picture.  shadow=\"black\"  draws the text again a pixel right and down in that colour ( shadowx  and  shadowy  move it);  outline=\"black\"  draws it in that colour at every pixel around it,  outlinewidth  pixels thick (1 to 3). both work with the built-in font and with bitmap fonts and turn and scale with the text. used together, the shadow falls from the outlined shape. they take no room in a layout, so leave padding for them, and use opaque colours: a see-through outline shows through where its copies overlap. \n  \n score: {score} \n  \n\n   built-in fonts \n   the engine has pixel fonts built in, so a game at a small resolution needs no font file: give  font  a  tse:  name on a  <text> , or on  <game>  for every text. each is drawn at its own height unless the text has a  size  (use a whole multiple of the line to keep pixels square). their letters keep their own widths, and their digits share one width, so a score does not shift as it counts. try several:  tse preview games/demo  shows each one on a game screen at 320 \u00d7 180, 480 \u00d7 270 and 640 \u00d7 360. \n    \n      screen  font  line (px)  look  \n      320 \u00d7 180   tse:small   6  tom thumb: 3 \u00d7 5, the smallest that reads  \n       tse:micro5   8  condensed, fits the most text a line  \n       tse:tiny5   8  wider and rounder  \n      480 \u00d7 270   tse:m5x7   10  m5x7: clean, with lower case and descenders  \n       tse:silkscreen   9  wide capitals, for titles and labels  \n       tse:caps   8  5 \u00d7 7 capitals only, retro  \n      640 \u00d7 360   tse:arcade   9  bold 8 \u00d7 8 arcade (press start 2p)  \n       tse:unscii   9  8 \u00d7 8 home-computer style  \n       tse:unscii-thin   9  the same, with thin strokes, lighter for body text  \n       tse:jersey10   14  tall and sporty, for hud numbers  \n       tse:unscii-tall   17  8 \u00d7 16, readable at length for dialogue  \n    \n  \n \n   the lantern keeper \n \n  \n   where each comes from and its license is in  native/fonts.md , which  tse package  puts beside a packaged game. \n\n    hud  \n   a layer drawn after the world that ignores the camera. coordinates are screen pixels (game pixels under a  screen ), top-left 0, 0. put it directly in the game's root. a  <fade>  goes here too. \n\n    camera  \n   \n     the camera follows its children:  <camera><player /></camera>  keeps the view on the player (several children: their middle; none: it stays put). \n     children are placed as if the camera were not there. its own  x  and  y  are an offset from the followed point. \n      zoom : 2 draws everything twice as large; must be positive. \n      bounds={[x, y, w, h]}  keeps the view inside a world area.  slide={0.4}  eases to new bounds (a room change) instead of jumping. \n      shake={shake}  takes a value from   useshake()  . \n     only the first camera counts. with none, the origin is the window's top-left. \n   \n "},{"id":"router","title":"Screens (Router)","page":"systems.html","group":"Systems","text":" \n   screens:  router  and  route  \n   a  router  shows one  route  at a time. only the shown route is in the world. \n  \nconst screen = usegame((s) => s.screen);        // \"title\" | \"play\" | \"end\", changed by game-state actions\n\n \n     \n     \n     \n \n{/* beside the router, so screen changes leave it alone */}\n \n  \n  \n// transitions.ts: a plain function of progress (0 to 1). fade out, switch behind the cover, fade in.\nimport type { transitionframe } from \"@tsengine/runtime\";\n\nexport function fadethrough(progress: number): transitionframe {\n  return progress   \n    \n      transition field  meaning  \n       fromx ,  fromy ,  tox ,  toy   where the screen being left or entered is, as an offset from its place.  \n       fromvisible ,  tovisible   whether each is drawn (and can be pressed). default true.  \n       cover   0 to 1: how covered the whole screen is by  covercolor . a fade through a colour.  \n    \n   \n     both screens are in the world during a transition. without  transition , screens switch at once. \n     a route's components are made when it comes and dropped when it goes, so each visit starts fresh (as with react router).  keepalive  keeps them and their state. \n      usemount  and  useunmount  in a screen mean \"the screen came\" and \"the screen went\". \n     the function may also take the two route names:  (progress, from, to) . \n   \n "},{"id":"tilemaps","title":"Tilemaps and movement","page":"systems.html","group":"Systems","text":" \n   tilemaps and movement \n   a  <tilemap>  draws a tiled map with no game object per tile. the same data is the solid grid your movement code asks about, so what is drawn and what blocks cannot disagree. \n   moving a component:  usebody  \n   a component that moves and is stopped by walls keeps a body: a box that moves a pixel at a time and is stopped by the solid tiles of a  <tilemap solid>  (tiles with the tiled property  solid ) and by every  <collider solid> , and says what it hit in the same step. a collider's  oncollisionenter  runs after the step's update, a step late for movement; a body does not wait. \n  \nexport function hero() {\n  const s = usemutable({ vy: 0 });\n  const { x, y, move, touching, place } = usebody({ x: 16, y: 32, width: 8, height: 11 });\n  useupdate((dt, input) => {\n    const dx = (input.down(\"right\") ? 1 : 0) - (input.down(\"left\") ? 1 : 0);\n    const hit = move(dx * 90 * dt, s.vy * dt);        // stopped at walls and floors, now\n    if (hit.down) s.vy = input.pressed(\"jump\") ? -220 : 0;\n    else s.vy += 600 * dt;\n    if (touching(\"spikes\")) place(16, 32);            // a collider tagged \"spikes\" overlaps it now\n  });\n  return    ;\n}\n\n// in the level:  , and   for a crate.\n  \n   \n      x  and  y  are state, in the component's parent's space (where an  <entity x y>  around it would put it), so the component renders where the body is. \n      move(dx, dy)  moves x first, then y, carrying fractions to the next move, and returns  { left, right, up, down, tags } : the sides it was stopped on and the tags of what stopped it ( \"tile\"  for a tile). \n      touching(tag) : whether a collider with that tag overlaps the body now. the component's own colliders do not count. \n      blocked(dx, dy) : whether something solid is where a move by (dx, dy) would put the body, without moving it.  blocked(0, 1)  is standing on the ground (a body standing still is not stopped by anything, so its last  move  says nothing of the floor);  blocked(facing, 0)  is a wall to jump off. in a handler,  x  and  y  are where the body is now, after any  move  earlier in the step; render draws it where the step left it. \n      overlapping(tag) : every collider with that tag overlapping the body now, as contacts with their  data  (typed by tag through  colliderdata ). a tower that never moves keeps a body as big as its reach to ask which enemies are near:  picktarget(overlapping(\"enemy\").map((e) => e.data)) . it sees the colliders where they were when the step's updates began. \n     bodies move in the order their components update, each against the world as it is at that moment. others see a body through a  <collider>  the component draws at its position. \n   \n   balls that pile up:  useball  \n   a body is moved by its own component. things that push each other, such as fruit piling up in a jar, marbles or a ball pit, are balls: circles the world steps together, once a step, before the step's updates. they fall under their gravity, push each other apart (the lighter one further) and are stopped by every  <collider solid>  and the solid tiles of a  <tilemap solid> . a pile is a list of components that each call  useball . \n  \n// colliders.ts: the data each tag carries, once for the game (balls use colliderdata, as colliders do).\ndeclare global {\n  interface colliderdata { fruit: { id: number; kind: string } }\n}\n\nexport function fruit({ id, kind, x, onmerge }: fruitprops) {\n  const { x: at, y, contacts } = useball({ x: x, y: 0, radius: 6, gravity: 600, tag: \"fruit\", data: { id: id, kind: kind } });\n  useupdate(() => {\n    const twin = contacts(\"fruit\").find((c) => c.data.kind === kind);   // the balls touching it now\n    if (twin) onmerge(id, twin.data.id);\n  });\n  return  ;\n}\n\n// the jar:   and two walls, then {fruits.map((f) =>  )}.\n  \n   \n      x  and  y  are the centre, as state, in the component's parent's space; they are copied from the ball each step, so the component renders where the ball is. \n     options:  radius ;  gravity  (pixels per second per second, downwards, default 0);  gravityx  (the same to the right, default 0: a tilted table, wind;  setgravityx(sideways)  changes it, as does a second argument to  setgravity );  mass  (default the radius squared);  friction  (how much of its sliding it loses each substep it is pressed against a solid, default 0.1);  drag  (how much of its speed it loses each second, default 0.2);  pinned  (start held in place, default false);  tag  and  data , which other balls see. \n      contacts(tag) : the balls with that tag touching this one now (within a pixel and a half, as a settled pile jitters), nearest first, each with its  tag ,  data  (typed by  colliderdata ),  owner ,  radius  and where its centre is. \n      push(vx, vy)  adds to its velocity in pixels per second (a blast);  place(x, y)  puts it somewhere at rest;  speed()  is how fast it moves, so a pile has settled when every ball is slow. \n      pin(true)  holds it where it is: it does not fall, nothing pushes it, and other balls pile up on it as on something solid (a frozen fruit, a peg);  pin(false)  lets it go, from rest. the  pinned  option starts it held.  setgravity(g)  changes its gravity: 0 hangs it in the air, a negative one floats it up. \n      stick(\"fruit\")  sticks it to every ball with that tag touching it now, and it stays stuck: the two are held together, touching, and move as one lump (honey, a magnet, a string of beads). call it every step to stick to whatever comes to touch it; it returns how many balls it is stuck to.  unstick()  lets go of them all, and a ball that leaves the world lets go by itself. \n     no bounce: piles settle rather than jitter. balls that start overlapping (two made in one place) pop apart at no more than 300 pixels per second, not across the world. \n     the step is deterministic, and balls are solved in an order worked out from where they are, so rewind and replays reproduce a pile exactly. while the world is paused or frozen, balls do not move. \n     balls touch balls and solids only: a collider does not see a ball. a component that should be hit by others draws a  <collider>  at its ball's position, as a body's does. shaped solid colliders count as their boxes. \n   \n   moving with plain functions \n   a  usebody  component and plain functions produce the same movement. kite's running, falling and jumping written as a component with  usebody  ( games/kite/src/runner.tsx ) puts the character on the same pixel every step as kite's rules do, through walls, ceiling corners and pits ( test/runner.test.ts ). choose by who else needs the movement. use a component when only it does, and plain functions when something else must run it too, such as a search or a solver that plays a room thousands of times without a world. \n   kite's plain movement functions read the same tile data as a  <tilemap> : \n  \n// rules/rooms.ts: runs once when imported, before the game starts.\n// tse-compile --scenes writes generated/tilemaps.ts from your .tmj files.\nimport { registertilemaps } from \"@tsengine/runtime\";\nimport { tilemaps } from \"../../generated/tilemaps.ts\";\nregistertilemaps(tilemaps);\n  \n  \n    // map = the .tmj file name; tiles from its tilesets' sheets\n  \n  \nimport { gettilemap, moveactorx, moveactory } from \"@tsengine/runtime\";\n\nconst grid = gettilemap(\"room1\");\nconst blocked = (x: number, y: number) => grid.solidatpixel(x, y);   // your box test\nmoveactorx(actor, vx * dt, blocked, (dir) => { actor.vx = 0; });      // a pixel at a time\n  \n    \n       tilegrid  method  returns  \n       solidat(col, row)   whether any layer's tile there is solid (the tile property  solid  in tiled).  \n       solidatpixel(x, y)   the same for a pixel of the map.  \n       tileat(layer, col, row)   the global tile id (0 when empty or outside).  \n       find(layer, gid)   every  [col, row, gid]  holding that tile: spawn points, markers.  \n       objects(type?)   the map's objects (of one class), with  x ,  y ,  width ,  height ,  name  and  properties .  \n       property(name)   one of the map's own custom properties.  \n    \n   \n     reading outside the map gives an empty cell, not an error. \n     kite puts gameplay data in a hidden layer ( markers : room start and exit tiles) and finds it with  find(\"markers\", gid) . only the visible  tiles  layer is drawn. \n     a map of any size costs what its view does: in the world, an unrotated, unscaled  <tilemap>  writes only the cells the camera shows (and one more cell round them). inside a  <hud> , or turned or scaled, it writes every cell. \n     a large map does not need splitting, but you can still mount part of it: kite maps over its room list and mounts the current room and its neighbours. \n      moveactorx  /  moveactory  move  { x, y, rx, ry }  a pixel at a time against  blocked(x, y) , carrying the sub-pixel remainder. on a block the remainder is dropped and  onblocked(direction)  runs; it may move the actor (a corner correction). \n     tilesets can be embedded, or external as json ( .tsj ) or xml ( .tsx ). without  image , a  <tilemap>  draws each tile from its own tileset's sheet, with its margin and spacing, so keep the sheets in the assets folder; a tile larger than a cell sits on the bottom of it, as in tiled. with  image , every tile is cut from that one sheet. \n     to use map objects as data, give an object layer the bool property  data  and its objects are for your rules, not prefabs:  gettilemap(\"room1\").objects(\"door\")  gives each one's position, size, name and custom properties, and the map's own properties are  property(name) . bramble reads its doors, people, items and enemies this way ( games/bramble/src/rules/maps.ts ), checking each one and naming the map and object when something is wrong. \n     importer limits: finite maps; csv or base64 layers (uncompressed, zlib or gzip, not zstd); not tilesets that are collections of images. each is reported when imported. \n   \n "},{"id":"scenes","title":"Scenes and prefabs","page":"systems.html","group":"Systems","text":" \n   scenes and prefabs \n   every exported component is a  prefab  that json scenes can place by name. \n  \n{\n  \"version\": 1,\n  \"entities\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"boss-1\",                          // unique in the scene, stable across edits\n      \"prefab\": \"enemy\",                       // exported component name\n      \"props\": { \"speed\": 2, \"tint\": \"blue\" }, // value props only\n      \"transform\": { \"x\": 120, \"y\": 40, \"z\": 1 },\n      \"children\": [ { \"id\": \"hat\", \"prefab\": \"hat\" } ]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n  \n  \nimport { loadscene } from \"@tsengine/runtime\";\nimport { prefabs } from \"./generated/prefabs.ts\";\n\nconst byid = loadscene(world, scene, prefabs);   // map of scene id \u2192 object, nested ones included\n  \n   \n     callbacks cannot come from data, and  children  is passed, not set. \n     scene children mount after the prefab's own, or at its  {children}  slot. \n     validate with  tse-compile src out --scenes scenes  ( tse1501\u20131509 ). tiled  .tmj  maps are imported too. \n     exported names must be unique across files ( tse1206 ). \n   \n "},{"id":"frame","title":"How a frame works","page":"systems.html","group":"Systems","text":" \n   how a frame works \n   \n     a new left click goes to the topmost  pointer  /  flex onpress  under the mouse. \n     each step runs  world.tick(dt, input)  (one step a frame, or as many as  fixedstep  holds): all  useinput  handlers, then all  useupdate  handlers, then every dirty component renders once, then structural changes commit (conditions and list items mount and unmount). \n     after each step, new touching pairs call  oncollisionenter  and separated ones  oncollisionexit . what they change renders at once, so the next step sees it and a run collides at the same steps at any frame rate. \n     to draw, the tree is walked depth-first into draw records in tree order (or  z  order), with the camera applied. a contact and what it caused are drawn in the same frame. \n     the backend draws and presents, then supplies next frame's input. \n   \n   what follows from this order \n   \n     a field written in a handler shows at the next render; the handler reads it back at once. \n     components see each other's changes a step apart. every update in a step sees the state as the step began, so a value a child reports to its parent in a step ( onmove(x) , then the parent's  setplayerx ) reaches its siblings, by props or through context, in the next step. that is one step of lag between independent things, and it repeats exactly. where it matters, a built-in reads its child directly, as  <camera>  follows what it holds. \n     objects attached mid-frame get hooks next frame. \n     with  fixedstep , steps 2 to 4 above run once per whole step, and drawing runs once per frame. see  input and timing . \n      freeze(n)  skips  useupdate  (not  useinput  or renders) for  n  steps. \n     any number of state writes in a frame means one render.  animatedsprite  costs no renders. \n     unchanged subtrees reuse last frame's draw records. \n     bad assets never crash: a missing image or unreadable colour draws magenta, with one warning. \n     colours are css:  #rgb ,  #rgba ,  #rrggbb ,  #rrggbbaa ,  rgb() ,  rgba() , named colours,  transparent . \n   \n "},{"id":"effects","title":"Effects and audio","page":"effects.html","group":"Effects and input","text":" \n   effects and audio \n   state is for what lasts. a burst of dust where you landed, or a sound, happens at a moment and then plays out. for those, you make a store once, mount its renderer once, and call it from anywhere, as with a toast library. there is no  useeffect . \n  \n// effects.ts\nimport { createparticles, createsounds } from \"@tsengine/runtime\";\n\nexport const fx = createparticles({\n  dust: { burst: 9, lifetime: 0.4, speed: 55, angle: 270, spread: 75, gravity: 160, fade: true },\n});\nexport const sfx = createsounds({ land: { src: \"land.wav\", volume: 0.5 }, start: \"start.wav\", shot: { src: \"shot.wav\", once: true } });\n  \n  \n{/* once, beside the camera (not inside it), so bursts are in world space */}\n \n\n// anywhere: a handler, a plain function, a game-state action\nfx.burst(\"dust\", x, y);               // options: { angle, count, color, endcolor }\nfx.burst(\"die\", x, y, { color: enemy.color });   // this burst in that colour; the preset's other bursts are as they were\nsfx.play(\"land\");                     // second argument: volume multiplier\n  \n   \n     a burst belongs to the layer, so it plays out even if whoever asked has left. with no layer mounted, it is counted but not drawn. \n     a preset is  <particles> 's props except  rate ,  emitting  and  space . \n     a burst's  color  and  endcolor  replace the preset's for that burst only, so one preset can make every enemy's death puff in that enemy's colour. one not given stays the preset's; a preset with no  endcolor  keeps a given  color  throughout. \n     a sound with  once: true  plays at most once a step however many components ask for it: twenty towers firing together make one sound. \n     continuous effects (a trail while dashing) stay in jsx:  <particles emitting={dashing} space=\"world\" /> . \n   \n\n   motion: springs and tweens \n  \nconst y = usespring(open ? 0 : -40, { duration: 0.4, bounce: 0.2 });       // follows its target like a spring\nconst opacity = usetween(visible ? 1 : 0, { duration: 0.25, ease: \"outcubic\" });\n \u2026 \n\n// to animate something in as it appears, start from a state that turns on once it is mounted:\nconst s = usemutable({ shown: false });\nusemount(() => { s.shown = true; });\nconst x = usespring(s.shown ? 0 : 24, { duration: 0.3, bounce: 0.15 });\n  \n   \n     each follows its target: change the target (from state or props) and the value moves to it. both start at their first target, without moving. \n      usespring  is set by  duration  (about the seconds it takes to settle, default 0.5) and  bounce  (0, the default, for no overshoot, up to just under 1 for a lot of it). a new target keeps the speed it had, so it turns round smoothly. \n      usetween  takes  duration  (default 0.25) and  ease :  linear ,  inquad ,  outquad ,  inoutquad ,  incubic ,  outcubic  (the default),  inoutcubic ,  insine ,  outsine ,  inoutsine ,  inback ,  outback ,  outelastic ,  outbounce , or a function from a  .ts  file. a new target starts again from where it is. \n     they step in the component's  useupdate : they follow the fixed step,  freeze  and replays, render only while moving, and keep their place through a hot reload. they also stop while  <game paused>  is on, so a pause menu does not animate. \n     each takes a number: a point is two springs, one for each axis. in plain code,  stepspring  and  steptween  (from  @tsengine/runtime ) take points too. \n      opacity={\u2026}  fades any element and what is in it; see  position and drawing order . \n   \n\n   music and fades \n  \n{/* plays while mounted: keep it outside any router */}\n \n\n{/* from={1} fades in at start */}\n   \n  \n\n   freeze frames, shake, squash \n   kite also uses  <particles>  directly, for room-wide drifting dust ( width ,  height ,  prewarm ,  space=\"world\" ) and dash sparks ( emitting={body.state === \"dash\"} ). its dash trail is a list of ghost  sprite s in state, with falling  opacity . \n   \n      freeze(steps) , called in a handler or in plain typescript (kite calls it from  playevents ), skips every  useupdate  for that many steps (a hit, a dash).  useinput  keeps running, and a press during the freeze is kept for the first step after. \n      useshake()  and  usesquash()  are state: see  custom hooks . \n   \n "},{"id":"layers","title":"Layers, light and blend modes","page":"effects.html","group":"Effects and input","text":" \n   layers, light and blend modes \n   a  <layer>  draws its children off screen, into a picture the size of the screen, and then lays that picture over what is under it with a  blend mode . that is how a cave goes dark and a lantern lights it again: a layer of near-black, multiplied over the world, with lights added into it where they shine. \n  \n{incave && (\n  // the dark, where no light reaches\n   \n    {/* bright in the middle, nothing at the edge */}\n     \n    {torches.map((t) =>  )}\n   \n)}\n  \n    <lighting ambient>  is a  <layer blend=\"multiply\" childblend=\"add\" clear={ambient}>  set up as a light map, and  <light radius color intensity>  a soft disc of colour falling off smoothly to nothing; lights that overlap add up. a light needs no image. anything else may go in the layer too: a sprite drawn in an add layer is a light of its shape. \n   \n      blend  is how the layer meets what is under it:  \"normal\"  paints over;  \"multiply\"  shades (white leaves it as it is, black makes it black);  \"add\"  brightens;  \"screen\"  brightens but never past white. \n      childblend  is how each child is drawn onto the layer itself:  \"add\"  makes two overlapping lights brighter than one. \n      clear  is what the layer holds before its children are drawn, every frame (transparent by default): the darkness of a light map. \n      opacity  fades the whole layer, and an opacity above it applies to the layer as a whole, once. \n     it is drawn through the same camera as the world (or the hud's view, inside a  <hud> ), so children are placed in world coordinates as usual. it covers the screen (the game's canvas under a  <screen> ). \n     inside a layer, children are drawn in  z  order among themselves, as they are anywhere else, and the layer as a whole is drawn at its own  z  among what is around it. layers nest four deep. \n     bramble's cave is the example:  games/bramble/src/play.tsx , the lantern, the items still lying there ( glowsof  in  rules/view.ts ) and the wisps as lights, and its picture in  stories/screenshots/play/cave.png . \n     lights do not cast shadows: walls do not block them. \n   \n   shaders \n   a layer can send each of its pixels through a  shader  as it is laid over: a flicker, a vignette, a ripple, a palette. the shader is a file under  assets/  holding one function,  effect , in glsl; the engine wraps it for the platform it runs on. \n  \n// assets/cave.glsl\nvec4 effect(vec2 uv) {\n  vec4 c = texture(image, uv);                        // the layer's own pixel\n  vec2 d = pixel / resolution - 0.5;                  // from the middle of the screen\n  float flicker = 1.0 - params[0] * (0.5 + 0.5 * sin(time * 11.0));\n  return vec4(c.rgb * flicker * (1.0 - 0.9 * dot(d, d)), c.a);\n}\n  \n  \n \u2026 \n  \n   \n      effect  is given  image  (the layer: read it with  texture(image, uv) ),  pixel  (where this pixel is, in screen pixels from the top left),  resolution  (the screen's size),  time  (the game's seconds, so a replay looks the same) and  params  (eight numbers from  params={[\u2026]} , the rest 0). what it returns is the pixel, which the layer's blend mode and opacity then use. \n     a missing file is a build error, with the nearest name, as a missing image is. glsl that does not compile is reported when the game starts, with the compiler's message and a line in your file, and the layer is drawn without it. \n     the software renderer that tests, previews and  tse screenshot  use runs no shaders: it draws the layer as it is and says so once.  tse screenshot --gpu  shows them. \n   \n   shaders on a sprite \n   a  <sprite>  or  <animatedsprite>  can send its own pixels through a shader as it is drawn, with the same kind of file. a layer's shader sees the whole screen; a sprite's sees the sprite, and knows how it is turned. that is what lighting needs when the light stays put and the sprite rolls: a ball lit from the top right of the screen stays lit there however it spins. \n  \n// assets/ball.glsl: lit from the top right of the screen, however the sprite is turned\nvec4 effect(vec2 uv) {\n  vec4 c = texture(image, uv);\n  if (c.a == 0.0) return c;\n  vec2 step = 1.0 / vec2(texturesize(image, 0));        // one of the image's pixels, in uv\n  vec2 light = transpose(turn) * normalize(vec2(1.0, -1.0)); // the screen's top right, in the image\n  // a pixel with nothing next to it toward the light is on the lit edge; with nothing toward the dark, on the shaded one.\n  float lit = texture(image, uv + light * step).a == 0.0 ? 1.0 : 0.0;\n  float dark = texture(image, uv - light * step).a == 0.0 ? 1.0 : 0.0;\n  return vec4(c.rgb * (1.0 + params[0] * (lit - dark)), c.a);\n}\n  \n  \n \n  \n   \n     besides what a layer's shader is given,  effect  gets  region  (the part of  image  drawn, as x, y, width and height in its pixels: one frame of a sheet),  turn  and  lookup . \n      turn  takes a direction in the image to the direction it is drawn on the screen (y down), with the sprite's rotation, its flips and the camera's. it never stretches, so  transpose(turn)  takes it back: a direction on the screen, found in the image. \n      lookup  is a second image, from  lookup=\"ramps.png\" : a colour table, read with  texture  or  texelfetch , so shading can step through colours you chose (warmer toward the light, cooler away from it) instead of only brightening and darkening. without one it is a white pixel. \n      time  is the game's seconds, as on a layer, so a shimmer or a pulse needs nothing from the game. a sprite with a shader is drawn afresh every frame because of it. \n     each shaded sprite is drawn on its own, so shade tens of sprites this way, not thousands. a missing file is a build error, as for a layer, and the software renderer draws the sprite as it is. \n   \n "},{"id":"input","title":"Input actions and timing","page":"effects.html","group":"Effects and input","text":" \n   input actions and timing \n   named actions \n   name actions once, and every  input.pressed / down / released  then accepts a name as well as a key. rebinding changes that one list. \n  \n// rules/controls.ts\nimport { createcontrols } from \"@tsengine/runtime\";\nexport const controls = createcontrols({\n  jump: [\"z\", \"space\", \"pada\"],\n  left: [\"arrowleft\", \"a\", \"padleft\"],\n});\n  \n  \nimport { controls } from \"./rules/controls.ts\";\n\n  \u2026          // must be an imported name (tse1210 otherwise)\n\nuseinput((input) => { if (input.pressed(\"jump\")) jump(); });\n  \n   \n      down : any key held.  pressed : one went down this step.  released : one went up with none still held. \n     an unknown name warns once and lists the actions. for rebinding at run time, see  saved data . there are no analog axes. \n     gamepad: the first pad's buttons and four directions are keys (see  key names ).  gamepaddeadzone  on  <game>  sets the stick threshold. \n   \n\n   forgiving input: buffers, coyote time, clicks a button took \n  \nimport { grace, usemutable, useupdate } from \"@tsengine/runtime/jsx\";\n\nconst s = usemutable({ coyote: 0, vy: 0 });\nuseupdate((dt, input) => {\n  // coyote time: still allowed to jump for 0.1 s after running off a ledge.\n  s.coyote = grace(onground, s.coyote, dt, 0.1);\n  // jump buffer: a jump pressed up to 0.1 s before landing still jumps.\n  if (s.coyote > 0 && input.pressedwithin(\"jump\", 0.1)) {\n    s.vy = -jump;\n    s.coyote = 0;\n    input.consumepress(\"jump\"); // one press, one jump\n  }\n  // a click that a hud button took is not also a click on the world.\n  if (input.pressed(\"mouseleft\") && !input.presshandled) placetower(input.pointerx, input.pointery);\n});\n  \n   \n      input.pressedwithin(key, seconds) : the key (or an action's key) went down within the last  seconds  of game time, this step included, and the press was not taken.  pressedwithin(key, 0)  is  pressed(key) . game time is the steps'  dt , so under  fixedstep  a window is whole steps and a replay repeats it. \n      input.consumepress(key)  takes the key's last press (each of an action's keys), so  pressedwithin  stops seeing it.  pressed  is not changed. \n      grace(active, left, dt, seconds)  returns  seconds  while  active , then counts down to 0: keep it in a field and pass it back each step. it is a plain function, so it also works in a rules file. \n      input.presshandled  is true on the step that sees a left click that a  <pointer> ,  <button>  or other pressable took.  input.pressed(\"mouseleft\")  is still true then: check both. \n   \n\n   who hears the keys:  inert  and  modal  \n   input is polled, so every  useinput  sees every key. two props of  <entity> ,  <flex>  and  <hud>  decide which subtrees hear it, as in html, so a component does not have to guard itself with an  if (paused) return . \n  \n// inert: this subtree hears nothing (no keys, no presses, no focus), but still updates and draws.\nreturn  \u2026 ;\n\n// modal: while it is mounted, everything outside it hears nothing, the screen behind it included.\nfunction pause() {\n  useinput((input) => { if (input.pressed(\"back\")) togglepause(); });   // it closes itself: the screen behind hears no escape\n  return  \u2026  ;\n}\n{paused &&  }\n  \n   \n      inert  is the html attribute. in the subtree,  useinput  and  useupdate  get no keys pressed or held, and pointers and buttons cannot be pressed, hovered, focused or reached by their  presskey . it still updates, draws and collides. \n      modal  is a  <dialog>  opened with  showmodal() : everything outside it is inert, its ancestors too, and the focus stays among its buttons. if several are mounted, the one mounted last takes the input; when it goes, the one below has it again. \n     a component passes them on as it would any prop, at its root:  function menu({ inert }: menuprops) { return <entity inert={inert}>\u2026</entity>; } , then  <menu inert={capturing} /> . (a state field cannot be named  inert  or  modal : they are the root's.) \n     it decides who hears input, not who updates: to stop the world stepping while a pause menu shows, give the root  <game paused={paused}>  (below). \n     a modal mounted during a step hears keys from the next step, so the press that opened it does not reach it. bramble's \"press a key\" prompt ( keyprompt.tsx ) is a modal with no children that takes the next key. \n   \n\n   fixed timestep \n  \n  \u2026  \n  \n   \n      useupdate  runs at a fixed rate with  dt = 1 / rate . a frame runs no step, one, or several (at most 8; leftover time is dropped). drawing stays once per frame. \n     each frame is drawn between the last two steps, by how far it is into the next one ( interpolate , on by default), so a 60-step game moves smoothly on a 120 hz or 144 hz screen. positions, rotations, scales and the camera's follow and slide are blended for drawing only:  useupdate , collisions and saves see whole steps. it draws up to one step behind. something that moves more than 64 pixels in one step (a respawn, a door) is drawn where it lands, not slid there.  <game interpolate={false}>  draws the latest step instead; a hidden window (tests,  tse screenshot ) does that by default. \n     a tap between two steps is not lost: the next step sees it as pressed and released. \n     the fixed step is off by default. without it, there is one update per frame, with the frame's own time. \n   \n   timers:  after  and  every  \n  \nimport { after, canceltimer, canceltimers, every } from \"@tsengine/runtime/jsx\";\n\noncollisionenter={(other) => {\n  canceltimer(s.flashtimer);                 // a second hit restarts the flash, and only the flash\n  s.flash = true;\n  s.flashtimer = after(0.15, () => { s.flash = false; });   // once, 0.15 s of game time from now\n}}\nusemount(() => {\n  every(1, () => { s.ticks += 1; });         // every second, until it unmounts\n});\ncanceltimers();                              // all of this component's timers\n  \n   \n     you call them as statements in a handler, as you would  settimeout  in react. the function you pass is a handler too: it writes state, calls actions, plays sounds. \n     a timer is the component's: it is cancelled when the component unmounts, so it never sets state on one that is gone. \n      after  and  every  return the timer's id (1, 2, 3\u2026 for the component, never 0, so  0  in state means none).  canceltimer(id)  stops that one timer and leaves the others; an id that is done, or 0, is ignored. \n     they count game time in the update phase: they follow the fixed step and replay, and stop during  freeze  and  <game paused> . \n     choosing between a timer and state: a timer is code, so it is not saved and a hot reload drops it (the log says so). use one for what is only for the eye: a flash, a blink, a beat before a title appears. a delay that is part of the game (a few seconds of safety after a hit, a slow) is state that counts down in  useupdate , or a time it ends at kept in state: it is then saved, reloaded and tested like any state. \n   \n   cutscenes: sequences \n   a cutscene is a list of steps run one after another as game time passes: a  sequence , written as data in a  .ts  file. a component keeps it in a field and steps it from  useupdate ; while it runs, the component can hold the world still. \n  \n// cutscenes.ts\nimport { call, easeout, sequence, tween, wait, type sequence } from \"@tsengine/runtime\";\n\nexport const nocutscene = (): sequence => sequence([]);   // what a field starts as: done already\nexport function caveintro(light: (reach: number) => void, say: (line: string) => void): sequence {\n  return sequence([\n    call(() => light(0)),\n    wait(0.4),\n    tween(1.2, light, easeout),                            // 0 to 1 over 1.2 s, eased\n    call(() => say(\"the lantern pushes the dark back.\")),\n    wait(1.8),\n    call(() => say(\"\")),\n  ]);\n}\n  \n  \nconst s = usemutable({ intro: nocutscene(), sawcave: false, reach: 1, line: \"\" });\nuseupdate((dt, input) => {\n  if (incave && !s.sawcave) {\n    s.sawcave = true;\n    s.intro = caveintro((reach) => { s.reach = reach; }, (line) => { s.line = line; });\n  }\n  if (s.intro.step(dt)) return;                            // the world waits while it plays\n  advance(readinput(input), dt);\n});\n  \n   \n     steps:  wait(seconds) ,  call(fn) ,  waitfor(() => done)  (the player closing a dialogue),  tween(seconds, set, ease)  (calls  set  with 0 to 1, ending on 1; eases  linear ,  easein ,  easeout ,  easeinout ),  parallel([\u2026]) , and  sequence([\u2026])  itself, which nests. \n      step(dt)  returns true while there is more to run. time left over when a step ends goes to the next, so a sequence takes what its steps add up to, whatever the step size; under the fixed timestep it plays the same every time, and replays. \n     start the field from a function ( nocutscene() ), not  null : the compiler lets a component call the methods of an object a function made for it, and types the field from what that function returns. \n      position  is where it is, as plain data, and  restore(position)  carries on from it: a save can keep a cutscene half played.  reset()  starts it over. \n     a test steps one without the engine:  const s = caveintro(\u2026); s.step(1); . a replay that crosses a cutscene waits it out (bramble's  test/fullrun.ts ). \n   \n\n   fast-forward and pause \n  \nconst speed = usegame((s) => s.speed);      // 1 or 2\nconst paused = usegame((s) => s.paused);\n  \u2026  \n  \n   \n      timescale={2}  runs two steps a frame, each with the same  dt : the game plays exactly as at 1, twice as fast, and a replay is the same at either speed. \n      paused  stops every  useupdate .  useinput , presses,  presskey  and the focus still work, so the pause menu's buttons unpause it. \n     unlike the other  <game>  settings, these two are read every frame, so they may be state. \n   \n\n   seeing inside a running game \n   \n     under  tse dev ,  f1  in the window ( <game overlaykey>  to change it) shows the layout overlay. it outlines every  <flex>  and its padding, every collider (a colour per layer) and every pointer, and labels the box under the mouse with the components it is in, its size and what sized it ( width grow ). a packaged game never has it. \n      tse dev  also prints the address of the inspector ( inspector: http://127.0.0.1:\u2026/ ): open it in a browser to see the tree by component, as react devtools shows it; the selected component's state, props, the game-state paths it reads and how many times it has rendered; and each game state with its last actions and what each one changed, as redux devtools shows them. \n     in the inspector you can change a state value or a game-state leaf by typing a json value. the change shows on the next render. an edit is not input, so a replay does not repeat it; the log says so each time. \n     the inspector's  profile  button times every component while the game runs. once a second it shows a table of milliseconds a frame: for each component (by name, with how many there are) its input, update and render time and renders a frame, the slowest first, and above it the frame's phases (steps, collisions, building the frame, drawing). a row over a millisecond is red. timing costs a little, so it is on only while the button is; in a test or a tool,  startprofiling()  from  @tsengine/runtime  gives the same report ( profiler.take() ). \n   \n\n   rewind \n   under  tse dev  the game records every frame's input and takes a snapshot every second, for the last five minutes. go back to the moment something went wrong, fix the code, and watch the same moment again. \n   \n      f2  pauses.  \u2190  and  \u2192  go back and on a frame,  \u2191  and  \u2193  a second; a bar over the game shows where you are.  f2  again plays on from there with live input, and the recorded future is dropped. the inspector's page has the same timeline, with a slider. \n     going to a frame restores the snapshot before it and plays the recorded input again to it, silently and without drawing: under a tenth of a second. \n     if you save a file while paused, the game is reloaded and the same input is played again with the new code, to the same frame, so you see the moment with the fix. \n      f4  saves the moment (from the snapshot before the paused frame to it) to  moments/ . a test plays it again with  replaymoment  from  @tsengine/player , so the bug becomes a replay test. \n     it relies on the game being a function of its input. each frame's state is hashed as it is played; when a replay differs from the recording ( math.random  or  date.now  in the game, a timer used for gameplay), rewind names the first frame that differs, in the log, on the bar and on the page. \n   \n  \nimport { replaymoment } from \"@tsengine/player\";\nimport moment from \"../moments/frame-420-1763000000000.json\" with { type: \"json\" };\n\ntest(\"the tower that missed now hits\", () => {\n  const states = replaymoment(gameconfig, moment, { stores: { usegame: usegame.store }, observe: () => usegame.getstate().score });\n  assert.equal(states.at(-1), 12);\n});\n  \n\n   testing without a window \n   \n      npm run check:kite  (or  check:<game> ) compiles, type-checks, runs tests and replays the game headless. \n     input scripts:  right*30, jump, right*20+dash . items run in order,  +  starts terms together,  name*n  holds n frames, a bare name taps,  _  waits. a key stays held until the script changes it, so  right, right  is one press held for two steps, and  right, _, right  is two presses. a name that is neither a key nor an action throws, listing the actions.  click@play  taps what is labelled play (the middle of the topmost thing that can be pressed and holds that text, as it is drawn when the frame comes up), so a script does not break when a button moves;  click@120,64  taps a point.  runscript  steps a game and returns what you observe.  playgame(gameconfig, { bindings })  plays one a piece at a time:  game.input(\"enter, _*5, space\")  runs a script, and  game.stepuntil(() => run().phase === \"pick\")  waits for the game instead of counting frames, so a test does not break the day something takes a little longer (it throws after  maxsteps , default 3600). a game exports  bindings  ( { jump: [\"z\"], \u2026 } ) so scripts can use action names. \n      tse screenshot <game> --script \"right*30\" --at 0,29  renders chosen steps to png through the software renderer (needs  npm run native:build:headless ). it compiles the game first, as  tse check  does, so a shot always shows the current source. if the game does not compile, it prints the errors and writes no shot. \n     for motion,  --every n --sheet name  writes every nth step and one contact sheet of them;  --from  and  --to  keep it to one stretch of a long run. \n      --at  counts back from the end with a negative number, and  last  is the last step:  --at 0,-30,last . a shot of how a script ends does not need its frames counted. \n      --setup file.ts  names a module whose default export  (player, step) => void  is called before each step. use it to put the game where the shot needs it (a later level, a full jar) without playing there;  findcomponent  from  @tsengine/testing  reaches a component's state. \n     a shot starts with no saved data and keeps none, so it looks the same whatever was played before and the player's saves are left alone.  --saves  uses the real ones. \n      --gpu  draws them with the desktop build on opengl instead, as a player's window does, so what a driver or a shader does shows. it needs  npm run native:build ; without a display it runs itself under  xvfb-run , which mesa's software opengl draws for. \n      --compare refs/  checks each frame against  refs/step-<n>.png  and fails if one differs;  --tolerance 64  lets a channel differ by that much (two mesa versions do), and  --update  writes the references.  npm run check:gpu  does this for the demo and kite, and ci runs it. \n   \n   testing one component:  @tsengine/testing  \n   as react testing library tests one react component,  render  mounts one compiled component in a headless world, and the test steps it, presses keys, reports collisions and presses, and reads what it holds and what it would draw. a component that owns its logic is then as easy to test as a rule. \n  \nimport { render } from \"@tsengine/testing\";\nimport { enemy } from \"../generated/enemy.ts\";\n\ntest(\"an enemy goes down after three shots\", () => {\n  const ondefeated = mock.fn();\n  const t = render(enemy, { hp: 3, ondefeated });\n  t.collide(\"enemy\", { tag: \"shot\", data: { damage: 1 } }, 3);   // its collider tagged \"enemy\", touched three times\n  assert.equal(ondefeated.mock.callcount(), 1);\n  assert.equal((t.state().s as { health: number }).health, 0);    // its own state, as its source names it (s.health)\n});\n  \n   \n      render(component, props, { tilemaps, controls, dt, collisions, keepgamestate }) : the game state is put back to its start, the maps are registered, and the component is mounted. so a test sets up the game state after  render , through its actions, or passes  keepgamestate: true  to keep what it set before. without  collisions: true  colliders never meet by themselves: a test reports the contacts it wants with  collide . with  collisions: true  each step finds its own contacts, as the player does, so a component whose children collide (a battle's enemies and shots) is tested whole. \n      place({ tag, data, x, y, width, height, onreceive })  puts another collider in the world, for the component to find or touch (an enemy near a tower); with  onreceive  the test sees each value the component  send s to it.  remove(collider)  takes it out. \n      send(tag, { x, y, radius }, value)  (or a  t.world.nearby(\u2026)  result in place of the circle) hands a value to the component's colliders of that tag, as another component's  send  would, and delivers it to their  onreceive  at once, without a step. \n      step(n)  runs steps with nothing pressed;  input(\"down, _, confirm\")  runs an input script, through the game's  controls  when given. \n      stepuntil(() => !play.s.active, { maxsteps })  runs steps until the condition holds (asked before the first and after each) and returns how many it ran; not done within  maxsteps  (default 3600) it throws, or with  throws: false  returns  maxsteps , for a balance run where a wave that never ends is a result to record. \n      collide(tagorcollider, { tag, data, width, height }, times)  and  separate(\u2026)  report contacts;  press(pointerorname)  clicks a pressable in its middle;  setprops(props)  gives new props;  unmount()  runs its unmount handlers. \n      state(component?)  is a component's state by the names in its source;  texts()  the words of every  <text> ;  find(nameorclass)  and  findall  search the tree. \n     outside  render , a whole-game test, a replay check or a balance tool reads the running game the same way:  findcomponent(play)  (or  findcomponent(\"hero\") ) is the component in the game's tree,  findcomponents  every match, and  componentstate(component)  its state by the names in its source, all from  @tsengine/testing . \n   \n   previews: one component on its own \n   as storybook shows one react component with given props,  tse preview  shows one component of the game, without playing to where it appears. a  stories/  folder beside  test/  holds a file for each component: its default export says how it is shown, and every other export is a story, its props. \n  \n// stories/sidepanel.stories.ts\nimport { stories, story } from \"@tsengine/player\";\nimport { sidepanel } from \"../generated/sidepanel.ts\";\nimport { usegame } from \"../src/gamestate.ts\";\n\nexport default stories(sidepanel, { size: [80, 180], scale: 3 });\n\nconst idle = { onstartwave: () => {}, onbuild: () => {}, onupgrade: () => {}, onsell: () => {} };\nexport const start = { gold: 120, lives: 20, wave: 0, active: false, tower: null, ...idle };\nexport const build = story(\n  { gold: 60, lives: 20, wave: 2, active: false, tower: null, ...idle },\n  { setup: () => usegame.setstate({ open: true }), input: \"_*60\" },   // game state first; let the slide-in finish\n);\n  \n   \n      stories(component, { size, origin, scale, background, wrap }) : the screen the component is drawn on ( size , in game pixels), where its own (0, 0) sits on it ( origin , default the top left), how many times larger a screenshot is ( scale , default 4), and  wrap  to put it inside something it needs, a  provider  or a  flex  that gives it a width. it is drawn with the game's own  <game>  look: background, assets, font, text size and colour, controls. \n      story(props, { setup, input })  for a story that sets game state first (every game state is put back to its start before  setup ) or plays an input script before it is looked at. a plain object is props only. stories are listed by name. \n      tse preview  opens a window on the first story, with hot reload: save the component or the stories file and it changes in place.  f5  and  f6  go to the previous and next story;  --story sidepanel  (or  sidepanel/build ) starts at one. \n      tse preview --screenshot  stores a png of every story in  stories/screenshots/ ; commit them.  tse check  then renders every story again and fails on a picture that changed, on a story with no picture, and on a picture whose story is gone, and says where the new pictures are. when the change is meant, store them again. this needs the headless libtse, which  @tsengine/native  ships; without it the check says it did not compare. \n   \n   hearth's stories found the build menu's  2 mortar 80  a few pixels wider than the side panel, which the game showed cut off at the window's edge. \n "},{"id":"saved","title":"Saved data and settings","page":"effects.html","group":"Effects and input","text":" \n   saved data and settings \n   a save and the player's settings are kept between runs in the game's folder in the user's data directory ( $xdg_data_home/<id> ,  ~/.local/share/<id> , or  $tse_data_dir  in tests). name the folder with the  id  prop of  <game>  (a literal: letters, digits,  _ ,  - ); without it nothing is kept. \n  \nimport { createsave, createsettings } from \"@tsengine/runtime\";\n\nexport const save = createsave({\n  name: \"save\", version: 2,\n  pick: () => { const s = usegame.getstate(); return { map: s.run.map, items: s.run.items }; },   // plain, json-safe\n  apply: (data) => usegame.getstate().continuefrom(data),\n  migrate: { 1: (old) => ({ ...old, opened: [] }) },        // version 1 -> 2\n});\nsave.write();  save.exists();  save.load();  save.remove();   // synchronous; call them from game-state actions\n\n// tree: true keeps every component's own state too (its usemutable fields), not only what pick takes.\nexport const run = createsave({ name: \"run\", version: 1, pick: () => ({ screen: usegame.getstate().screen }), apply: (d) => usegame.actions().show(d.screen), tree: true });\n// apply goes through an action: setstate is for tests and tools, and is an error while an action (the one calling load) runs.\n\nexport const settings = createsettings({                // each setting's type is its default's: `fullscreen` is a boolean\n  name: \"settings\", controls,\n  defaults: { music: 0.6, effects: 0.8, fullscreen: false, bindings: null as record  | null },\n});\nsettings.get();  settings.set({ music: 0.4 });  settings.reset();\n\n// show them: the settings screen reads them, and follows them while it is shown.\nconst music = usestore(settings, musicvolume);      // musicvolume() { return settings.get().music; } in a .ts file\n  \n   \n     with  tree: true , component state is saved too: an enemy that keeps its own  hp  in  usemutable  keeps it in the save. on  load ,  apply  runs first; once the tree it builds has settled, each component takes its saved state back, found by its path from the root, and a list item by its  key  (so an enemy removed before the save is loaded does not hand its state to the next one). values that are not plain data (a function, a body) are left out, said once. \n     a save is stored as json,  { version, data }  (and  tree  with  tree: true ). a file that does not parse is said once and read as no save; a save from a  newer  version than the game is refused with a message;  migrate  steps run in order up to the current version. \n     settings are applied when the game starts, before the first frame, and on each  set :  music  and  effects  are the two volume groups ( setvolume(\"music\", v)  multiplies every  <music> ;  effects  every sound),  fullscreen  switches the window,  bindings  goes to  controls.load . other settings are the game's own. \n      settings  and  controls  are not state a component reads, so  settings.subscribe(fn)  (after each  set ,  reset  and reload, once applied) and  controls.subscribe(fn)  (after each  bind ,  reset  and  load ) say when they change; both return the function that stops it. a component shows them with  usestore(settings, read) , the counterpart of react's  usesyncexternalstore .  read  is a function from a  .ts  file that returns what to show. it runs at mount and on each notification, the component renders only when the data it returns differs, and the subscription ends when the component goes (bramble's  settings.tsx ). underneath it is  usemount  returning the subscription's  cleanup , which a component can also write itself. if several screens show a setting, mirror it into the game state from a  .ts  module instead. \n     to rebind, use  controls.bind(\"jump\", [\"space\"])  (false for an unknown action or a key that is not a key name),  controls.reset() ,  controls.tojson() ,  controls.load(data)  (ignores unknown actions and keys). the change applies at once.  input.lastpressed()  is the first key pressed this step, or  undefined ; it is how you write \"press a key for jump\" ( const key = input.lastpressed(); if (key !== undefined) bindkey(action, key); ). \n     in a runtime test, call  setstorage(creatememorystorage()) ;  new recordingbackend({ storage: { save: \"\u2026\" } })  starts a replay from a save, and  backend.stored  shows what was written. \n   \n "},{"id":"elements","title":"Built-in elements","page":"reference.html","group":"Reference","text":" \n   built-in elements \n   built-ins are capitalised. required props are marked  required . \n    \n    \n "},{"id":"diagnostics","title":"Error codes","page":"reference.html","group":"Reference","text":" \n   error codes \n   codes are stable and never renumbered. \n    \n "},{"id":"gotchas","title":"Gotchas and tips","page":"reference.html","group":"Reference","text":" \n   gotchas and tips \n   \n     if nothing moves, check that you multiplied by  dt  and that the component is mounted. \n     a change to a list item through another name is refused ( const e = s.list[0]; e.hp = 0 , or a loop variable). write through the field ( s.list[0].hp = 0 ), or give it a new list:  s.list = s.list.map(\u2026) .  s.list.push(x)  itself is fine. \n     a callback under  {s.x !== null && \u2026}  does not narrow  s.x . take  const x = s.x  above it and read  x : typescript keeps a  const 's narrowing in a closure. \n      <game>  settings are literals or constants imported from a  .ts  module ( width={640} ,  width={screen_w} , never state), and  <game>  is only ever a root. \n      <text>  takes its words as children ( score: {score} ). a  text=  attribute is an error ( tse1203 ), and the editor's quick fix moves it into the children. \n     if a collider never fires, check both sides: each one's  hits  must allow the other's  layer , and neither may be in a  <hud> . \n     for sharp pixel art, keep  smooth  off and use  screen fit=\"integer\" . \n      f11  or  alt + enter  goes full screen;  fullscreenkey={false}  disables f11. \n     an effect store does nothing until you mount its  <particlelayer> , next to the  camera  rather than inside it. \n     changing an imported constant while the game runs updates nothing on screen: no component re-renders for it. use  usegame  for a value that changes. \n     to debug a hitbox, put a translucent  shape  of the same size beside it. \n   \n "}];
